(globalThis.TURBOPACK||(globalThis.TURBOPACK=[])).push(["object"==typeof document?document.currentScript:void 0,80873,e=>{"use strict";let t="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules",o={cloudComputing:{title:"Describe cloud computing",url:`${t}/describe-cloud-compute/`},cloudBenefits:{title:"Describe the benefits of using cloud services",url:`${t}/describe-benefits-use-cloud-services/`},cloudServiceTypes:{title:"Describe cloud service types",url:`${t}/describe-cloud-service-types/`},coreArchitecture:{title:"Describe the core architectural components of Azure",url:`${t}/describe-core-architectural-components-of-azure/`},compute:{title:"Describe Azure compute services",url:`${t}/describe-azure-compute-networking-services/`},networking:{title:"Describe Azure networking services",url:`${t}/describe-azure-networking-services/`},storage:{title:"Describe Azure storage services",url:`${t}/describe-azure-storage-services/`},identity:{title:"Describe Azure identity, access, and security",url:`${t}/describe-azure-identity-access-security/`},cost:{title:"Describe cost management in Azure",url:`${t}/describe-cost-management-azure/`},governance:{title:"Describe features and tools in Azure for governance and compliance",url:`${t}/describe-features-tools-azure-for-governance-compliance/`},manageDeploy:{title:"Describe features and tools for managing and deploying Azure resources",url:`${t}/describe-features-tools-manage-deploy-azure-resources/`},monitoring:{title:"Describe monitoring tools in Azure",url:`${t}/describe-monitoring-tools-azure/`}},a=[{slug:"cloud-computing",order:1,title:"What cloud computing actually is",emoji:"☁️",domain:"cloud",minutes:16,summary:"Cloud computing is renting compute, storage, and services over the internet — plus how you pay for it, and what serverless really means.",examFocus:"AZ-900 wants you to contrast buying hardware (CapEx) with paying for what you use (consumption / OpEx), name the pricing models, and know that serverless still uses servers — you just do not manage them.",youWillLearn:["A plain-English definition of cloud computing","Why CapEx, OpEx, and consumption-based billing are different","Pay-as-you-go vs reserved vs spot, at fundamentals level","What serverless is — and what it is not"],officialModule:{...o.cloudComputing,units:["What is cloud computing","Describe the consumption-based model"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"💡",text:"Cloud computing is not magic, and it is not \"someone else's computer\" as a joke. It is a business model: instead of buying servers, a building, cooling, and a night-shift operator, you rent computing over the internet and pay for what you use. Azure is Microsoft's version of that rental shop."},{type:"p",text:"Picture electricity. You do not build a power plant behind your house. You plug in, the grid supplies capacity, and the bill tracks how much you used. Cloud computing does the same thing for virtual machines, storage, databases, networks, and software. Microsoft Learn's definition is: the delivery of computing services over the internet, typically with a pay-as-you-go price."},{type:"think",text:"Cloud = rent computing like you rent electricity. You do not own the plant."},{type:"heading",text:"What you are actually renting"},{type:"p",text:'Those "computing services" are not one product. They include servers (compute), disks and blobs (storage), databases, networking, software, analytics, and AI. You request them on demand, often in minutes, from a shared pool of resources that Microsoft already built and runs in datacenters around the world.'},{type:"list",items:["On-demand: you can start a VM on a Tuesday afternoon without ordering hardware","Shared pool: many customers share Microsoft's fleet, isolated from each other","Measured: usage is metered — hours, gigabytes, transactions, executions","Elastic: capacity can grow and shrink without you buying a new rack","Self-service: a portal, CLI, or API, not a ticket to a datacenter team"]},{type:"example",title:"Northwind Traders, before and after",body:"Northwind used to wait eight weeks for a new database server, then pay for it whether the holiday sale happened or not. In Azure they create a database in minutes, scale it for Black Friday, and scale it back in January. Same business. Completely different relationship with hardware."},{type:"diagram",title:"Own a plant vs plug into the grid",ascii:`Traditional IT                         Cloud
---------------                        -----
Buy servers                            Request a service
Build a datacenter                     Microsoft already built it
Hire people to patch, cool, guard      Microsoft operates the plant
Pay whether it is busy or idle         Metered bill for what you used

        You  ──plug in──>  Azure grid`},{type:"heading",text:"CapEx, OpEx, and the consumption-based model"},{type:"p",text:"This is where AZ-900 starts scoring easy points. Companies spend money in two big buckets. Capital expenditure (CapEx) is a large upfront purchase of an asset — servers, racks, a building — that you depreciate over years. Operating expenditure (OpEx) is money you spend to run the business this month — electricity, salaries, a cloud bill."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"CapEx",subtitle:"Buy the asset",points:["Big upfront spend: servers, datacenter, SAN, cooling","You own it, whether it sits idle or not","Slow to change — you guessed demand years ahead","Classic on-premises model"]},{title:"OpEx",subtitle:"Pay to operate",points:["Ongoing costs: subscriptions, power, cloud invoices","Usually easier on cash flow than a giant purchase","Cloud bills are OpEx"]},{title:"Consumption-based",subtitle:"A style of OpEx",points:["You pay for what you actually used","No (or very little) unused capacity sitting on the floor","Stop the resource, stop most of the bill"]}]},{type:"example",title:"Gym membership vs paying at the door",body:"A monthly gym membership is a fixed OpEx: you pay even if you never go. Paying only on the days you walk in is consumption-based. Most Azure meters work like the door fee — VM hours, gigabytes stored, function executions — not like a membership you cannot pause."},{type:"think",text:"CapEx = buy the oven. OpEx = pay each month. Consumption = pay per pizza you actually bake."},{type:"trap",text:'Cloud is not automatically cheaper. It is more flexible. A VM you leave running 24/7 for three years can cost more than buying a box. The win is not running it when you do not need it — and not buying a warehouse of boxes "just in case."'},{type:"heading",text:"Cloud pricing models you must be able to compare"},{type:"p",text:'"Consumption-based" is the idea. Azure then offers a few common ways to buy that capacity. At AZ-900 level you do not need rate-card math. You need to match a customer\'s situation to the right model.'},{type:"table",caption:"Three models the exam loves to mix up",headers:["Model","The deal","Best when","Watch-out"],rows:[["Pay-as-you-go","List price, billed for what you use, no term","Unpredictable or short-lived work; experiments","Highest unit price if it runs all year"],["Reserved","Commit for 1 or 3 years to a size/region (or similar commitment)","Steady production that will run anyway","You still pay if you over-commit and stop using it"],["Spot","Unused Azure capacity at a deep discount","Batch, rendering, tests — work that can be interrupted","Azure can evict the VM when it needs the capacity back"]]},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["Pay-as-you-go = flexibility, no commitment","Reserved = discount in exchange for a time commitment","Spot = cheapest compute, but interruptible","Consumption-based is the umbrella idea: meter usage instead of buying hardware"]},{type:"trap",text:'Reserved is not the same as Spot. Reserved is a commitment you choose so Microsoft can plan capacity. Spot is leftover capacity Microsoft might take back. One is "I promise to stay." The other is "I will leave if you need the chair."'},{type:"heading",text:"Serverless: you write code, the cloud runs it"},{type:"p",text:"Serverless does not mean there are no servers. It means you do not provision, patch, or scale those servers. You give Azure a piece of code (or a workflow), Azure hosts it, Azure scales it from zero to busy and back, and on a consumption plan you pay when it runs. Azure Functions is the poster child. Logic Apps is the workflow cousin."},{type:"example",title:"PixelForge Games resizes screenshots",body:"Every time a player uploads a screenshot, a function fires, resizes the image, and stores a thumbnail. At 3 a.m. the function sits at zero instances and costs almost nothing. At launch day it scales out by itself. Nobody on PixelForge's team picked a VM size or installed IIS.",code:"// Mental model, not syntax to memorize\n// Event happens → Azure runs your function → you pay for that execution"},{type:"think",text:"Serverless = you own the code. Azure owns the server, the OS, and the scale-out."},{type:"trap",text:'Serverless is not "free," and it is not IaaS. If the question says you must manage the operating system, that is not serverless. If it says you deploy a VM and install your app, that is not serverless either.'},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Cloud computing",what:"Rent compute/storage/services over the internet",remember:"Electricity grid, not a home generator"},{term:"CapEx",what:"Upfront spend on assets you own",remember:"Buy the servers"},{term:"OpEx",what:"Ongoing operating cost",remember:"The monthly cloud bill"},{term:"Consumption-based",what:"Pay for metered usage",remember:"Stop it, stop paying (mostly)"},{term:"Pay-as-you-go",what:"No commitment, list-ish rates",remember:"Flexible, pricier per hour"},{term:"Reserved",what:"1- or 3-year commitment discount",remember:"Steady workloads"},{term:"Spot",what:"Unused capacity, can be evicted",remember:"Cheap and interruptible"},{term:"Serverless",what:"You write code; Azure runs and scales it",remember:'Azure Functions, not "no computers"'}]},{type:"onesentence",text:'Cloud computing is renting metered IT like electricity: you trade CapEx for OpEx, pick pay-as-you-go, reserved, or spot depending on how committed and interruptible you are, and serverless is the extreme of "just run my code."'},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"Fabrikam runs a nightly report that can be restarted if it fails. They want the lowest possible compute price and can tolerate the job being stopped mid-run. They do not want a one- or three-year commitment.",answer:"Spot (interruptible unused capacity)",why:'Pay-as-you-go would work but costs more. Reserved is a commitment they do not want. Spot is designed for work that can be evicted. Serverless could also fit some jobs, but the exam cue "lowest price" plus "can be interrupted" is Spot.'}],quiz:[{id:"cc-1",prompt:"Which statement best defines cloud computing for AZ-900?",options:["Installing Windows Server on hardware you purchased for the office","Delivering computing services over the internet, typically billed for what you use","A private network that never connects to the internet","A single physical server that several departments share in a closet"],answer:1,explanation:"Microsoft describes cloud computing as delivering compute, storage, databases, networking, software, and more over the internet, usually on a pay-as-you-go or consumption basis.",trap:"Sharing one office server is not cloud. Cloud is an on-demand pool of services, not a dusty tower under someone's desk."},{id:"cc-2",prompt:"A company buys racks of servers and a cooling system for a new datacenter. What kind of spending is that?",options:["OpEx","Consumption-based billing","CapEx","Spot pricing"],answer:2,explanation:"Buying assets up front is capital expenditure (CapEx). A monthly Azure invoice is OpEx. Consumption-based is paying for metered use of a service, not buying the rack."},{id:"cc-3",prompt:"What is the main idea of a consumption-based model?",options:["You pay a fixed annual fee whether you use the service or not","You pay only for the resources you consume","You must buy hardware that Microsoft then manages","You prepay three years and cannot change the VM size"],answer:1,explanation:"Consumption-based means the bill tracks usage — hours, gigabytes, executions. A fixed fee regardless of use is the opposite. A three-year lock is reserved pricing, which is a different model."},{id:"cc-4",prompt:"Tailwind Traders will run the same production VM size in the same region for the next three years. They want the lowest predictable price and can commit. What should they look at first?",options:["Spot VMs","Pay-as-you-go only","Reserved pricing (a 1- or 3-year commitment)","Building their own datacenter to avoid Azure fees"],answer:2,explanation:"Reserved pricing trades a time commitment for a discount on steady workloads. Spot can be cheaper still but is interruptible — a bad default for production that must stay up. Pay-as-you-go is flexible but usually costs more per hour."},{id:"cc-5",prompt:"Why would you choose Spot VMs instead of pay-as-you-go?",options:["You need a legally binding uptime SLA for a customer-facing storefront","You want unused Azure capacity at a discount and can handle eviction","You want Microsoft to patch the guest OS for you automatically","You want to convert CapEx into a building purchase"],answer:1,explanation:"Spot uses spare Azure capacity. The discount is the point; eviction is the trade-off. It is not a security feature and it is not how you get OS patching (that is more of a PaaS/SaaS conversation).",trap:'Do not pick Spot for "must never go down" workloads. That is the classic exam trap.'},{id:"cc-6",prompt:"A developer wants to run small pieces of code when blobs arrive in storage, without creating or patching servers. Which idea is that?",options:["CapEx","A reserved physical rack in a colocation cage","Serverless (for example Azure Functions)","Buying a hypervisor and running it in the office"],answer:2,explanation:"Serverless means you write the code and the cloud runs it. You do not manage the servers. Azure Functions on a consumption plan is the AZ-900 example."},{id:"cc-7",prompt:"Which pair correctly matches the spending type with an example?",options:["CapEx = monthly Azure invoice; OpEx = buying a SAN","CapEx = buying servers up front; OpEx = paying monthly for Azure usage","CapEx and OpEx are the same in the cloud","OpEx = a 10-year datacenter lease paid in one go"],answer:1,explanation:"CapEx is up-front spending on physical assets that depreciate. OpEx is ongoing spending on services as you use them — the default cloud model."},{id:"cc-8",prompt:"In serverless computing, what does the customer NOT have to manage?",options:["The application code","The servers and infrastructure the code runs on","The data the code processes","Who is allowed to call the function"],answer:1,explanation:"Serverless still runs on servers — you just never see or manage them. You still own the code, the data, and access decisions.",trap:"“Serverless” does not mean “no servers exist.” It means “no servers you manage.”"},{id:"cc-9",prompt:"Which benefit of the consumption-based model helps a startup most when it is not sure the app will succeed?",options:["No up-front hardware cost, and you can stop paying when you stop using it","A guaranteed 100% SLA on every service","Free outbound bandwidth forever","Microsoft writes the application code"],answer:0,explanation:"Pay-for-what-you-use removes the up-front gamble. If the idea fails, you delete resources and stop paying — no racks to sell."},{id:"cc-10",prompt:"Which statement about cloud pricing models is correct?",options:["Reserved capacity is cheaper than pay-as-you-go because you commit for 1 or 3 years","Spot is the best choice for workloads that must never stop","Pay-as-you-go always requires a 3-year contract","Serverless is billed per physical server you rent"],answer:0,explanation:"Reservations trade commitment for a discount. Spot is cheap but interruptible. Pay-as-you-go has no commitment. Serverless bills per execution or resource consumed."}]},{slug:"shared-responsibility",order:2,title:"The shared responsibility model",emoji:"🤝",domain:"cloud",minutes:18,summary:"Security in Azure is a split: Microsoft always owns the physical datacenter, you always own your data and access, and everything in between slides depending on IaaS, PaaS, or SaaS.",examFocus:"The exam will name a layer — physical hosts, operating system, application, data, identities — and ask who is responsible. If you can shift the line for IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS, you will catch questions other people guess.",youWillLearn:['Why "the cloud is secure" is the wrong sentence',"What Microsoft always owns vs what you always own","How the split changes for IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS",'How to answer "who patches the OS?" style questions'],officialModule:{...o.cloudComputing,units:["Describe the shared responsibility model"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🏢",text:"Moving to Azure does not mean Microsoft becomes your CISO. Think of an apartment building. The landlord owns the structure, the locks on the front door, the electricity, and the cameras in the lobby. You still lock your own flat, decide who gets a key, and choose whether you leave your laptop on the kitchen table. Shared responsibility is that split, written for cloud."},{type:"p",text:'Microsoft\'s model is simple once you stop treating "security" as one blob. Some duties never leave Microsoft. Some duties never leave you. A middle band — operating systems, network controls, applications — moves toward Microsoft as you go from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS.'},{type:"think",text:"Microsoft secures the cloud. You secure what you put in the cloud — especially data and access."},{type:"heading",text:"The layers that never move"},{type:"p",text:'Start with the extremes. If you remember only two lists, remember these. They show up on the exam as "always" questions.'},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Always Microsoft",subtitle:"The building",points:["Physical datacenters (buildings, cages, cameras, guards)","Physical network (cables, core routers, backbone)","Physical hosts (the actual servers under the hypervisor)"]},{title:"Always you (the customer)",subtitle:"Your flat",points:["Information and data — classification, encryption choices, backups you configure","Accounts and identities — who can sign in, MFA, guest users","Devices and endpoints that your people use","Access management — keys, roles, sharing links"]}]},{type:"diagram",title:"The stack, from dirt to data",ascii:`YOU always                  DATA, identities, access, devices
        ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~
moves with IaaS/PaaS/SaaS   Application
                            Runtime / middleware
                            Operating system
                            Network controls
        ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~
MICROSOFT always            Physical hosts
                            Physical network
                            Physical datacenter`},{type:"trap",text:'"Azure is responsible for security" is too vague to be the answer. Ask: security of what? The building? Yes. Your blob of payroll files and the intern\'s password? That is still you.'},{type:"heading",text:"The line moves with the service type"},{type:"p",text:"IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS are taught in the next service-types lesson. For responsibility, you only need this: the more Microsoft provides as a finished service, the more Microsoft patches. You never get to dump data or identity onto them."},{type:"table",caption:"Who typically manages each layer",headers:["Layer","IaaS (VM)","PaaS (App Service / Azure SQL)","SaaS (Microsoft 365)"],rows:[["Physical datacenter, network, hosts","Microsoft","Microsoft","Microsoft"],["Operating system","You","Microsoft","Microsoft"],["Runtime / middleware","You","Microsoft","Microsoft"],["Application","You","You (your code) / Microsoft (platform)","Microsoft"],["Data","You","You","You"],["Identities and access","You","You","You"]]},{type:"interactive",diagram:"shared-responsibility"},{type:"example",title:'Contoso\'s three different "servers"',body:"Contoso lifts a Windows VM into Azure (IaaS): they still install patches, antivirus, and IIS. They move a web API to App Service (PaaS): Microsoft patches the OS and the web stack; Contoso still writes secure code and locks down who can call the API. They give staff Outlook on Microsoft 365 (SaaS): Microsoft runs Exchange; Contoso still decides who has a mailbox and whether mail can be forwarded to personal Gmail."},{type:"think",text:"IaaS = you patch the OS. PaaS = Microsoft patches the OS. SaaS = Microsoft runs the app. You always own the data and the keys to the door."},{type:"heading",text:"Physical datacenter vs data vs access vs OS vs application"},{type:"p",text:"Exam writers love to name one layer and wait. Walk the stack out loud. Physical datacenter: Microsoft, always — you cannot wander into an Azure region with a badge printer. Data: you, always — Microsoft will not decide your retention policy or whether that spreadsheet is confidential. Access: you — Entra ID roles, shared links, storage keys, SAS tokens. OS: you on a VM, Microsoft on App Service. Application: you if you wrote it, Microsoft if you are using theirs (Outlook, Teams, Dynamics)."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["Customer always: data, endpoints, accounts, access","Microsoft always: physical datacenter, physical network, physical hosts","On IaaS you still manage the guest OS","On PaaS Microsoft manages OS and runtime; you manage your app and data","On SaaS Microsoft manages the app; you still manage data and identities"]},{type:"steps",items:[{title:'If the question says "physical datacenter" or "host hardware"',text:"Microsoft. Every service type."},{title:'If it says "guest operating system" on a virtual machine',text:"You. That is IaaS. You patch Windows or Linux."},{title:'If it says "App Service" or "Azure SQL" and "patch the OS"',text:"Microsoft. That is PaaS."},{title:'If it says "who is responsible for the data in Microsoft 365?"',text:"You. SaaS does not donate your data to Microsoft's responsibility list."},{title:'If it says "who configures MFA and guest access?"',text:"You. Identities and access stay with the customer."}]},{type:"trap",text:'Do not confuse "Microsoft offers encryption features" with "Microsoft is responsible for your data." The platform gives you tools (encryption at rest, Key Vault, sensitivity labels). You choose to use them, you classify the data, you grant access. Tools are not a transfer of ownership.'},{type:"diagram",title:"Apartment mapping (keep this in your head)",ascii:`Landlord (Microsoft)              Tenant (you)
--------------------              -------------
Building and cameras              What you store in the flat
Electricity and plumbing          Who gets a key (identities)
The hallway locks                 How you share files
The physical server room          Your app code (IaaS/PaaS)
Hypervisor / host OS              Guest OS on a VM (IaaS only)`},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Shared responsibility",what:"Security duties split between Microsoft and you",remember:'Not "Azure handles security"'},{term:"Physical datacenter",what:"Buildings, power, guards, racks",remember:"Always Microsoft"},{term:"Data",what:"The information you store and process",remember:"Always the customer"},{term:"Access / identities",what:"Accounts, roles, keys, MFA",remember:"Always the customer"},{term:"OS on a VM",what:"Windows or Linux guest",remember:"Customer (IaaS)"},{term:"OS on App Service",what:"The platform's OS",remember:"Microsoft (PaaS)"},{term:"SaaS application",what:"Outlook, Teams, Dynamics",remember:"Microsoft runs the app; you still own data"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Microsoft always owns the physical world under Azure, you always own your data and who can reach it, and the operating system and application slide toward Microsoft as you move from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"Adventure Works deploys a Linux virtual machine in Azure and later moves the same workload to Azure App Service. After the move, who is responsible for applying Linux security patches?",answer:"Microsoft, once it is App Service (PaaS). On the VM it was Adventure Works.",why:"The guest OS is a customer job on IaaS. App Service is PaaS, so Microsoft maintains the OS and runtime. Adventure Works still owns the app code, the data, and access control either way."},{type:"scenario",title:"Another exam-style scene",setup:'A manager says, "We use Microsoft 365, so Microsoft is responsible if an employee shares a confidential customer list with the wrong person." Is that right?',answer:"No. Sharing and access are customer responsibilities even on SaaS.",why:"Microsoft runs Exchange and SharePoint. The customer still decides sharing policies, guest access, and whether that list should have been there. Data and access never leave the customer column."}],quiz:[{id:"sr-1",prompt:"In the shared responsibility model, which of these is always Microsoft's responsibility?",options:["Classifying which files are confidential","Physical datacenter security","Choosing who is a Global Administrator","Deciding retention for HR records"],answer:1,explanation:"Physical datacenters, physical networks, and physical hosts are always Microsoft. Data classification, admin roles, and retention are always the customer."},{id:"sr-2",prompt:"You run a Windows virtual machine in Azure (IaaS). Who is responsible for installing Windows security updates on that VM?",options:["Microsoft, because the VM is in Azure","You, the customer","The hardware vendor who built the host","Nobody — VMs patch themselves with no configuration"],answer:1,explanation:"IaaS means you get a VM and you manage the guest operating system, including patches. Microsoft maintains the physical host and hypervisor underneath.",trap:'"It is in Azure, so Microsoft patches it" is the #1 shared-responsibility trap. That sentence is true for PaaS/SaaS OS layers, not for your VM.'},{id:"sr-3",prompt:"A company hosts a web app on Azure App Service (PaaS). Who typically patches the operating system?",options:["The customer, every Patch Tuesday","Microsoft","The customer's ISP","Shared equally, so both must log into the VM"],answer:1,explanation:"App Service is PaaS. There is no guest OS for you to RDP into. Microsoft manages OS and runtime. You still manage your application code, configuration, and data."},{id:"sr-4",prompt:"Which responsibility stays with the customer even when they use Microsoft 365 (SaaS)?",options:["Keeping Exchange Online servers online","Physical access to Microsoft's datacenters","Their data and who is allowed to access it","Patching the Outlook service's operating system"],answer:2,explanation:"SaaS shifts the application and OS to Microsoft. Data, accounts, and access management remain with the customer."},{id:"sr-5",prompt:"Fabrikam stores customer photos in Azure Blob Storage. Microsoft encrypts storage at rest. Who is still responsible for not granting public anonymous access to that container?",options:["Microsoft, because encryption is on","Fabrikam, because access control is a customer duty","The end customers who uploaded the photos","Azure Policy, automatically, with no configuration"],answer:1,explanation:"Platform encryption does not decide your sharing. Access — public containers, SAS tokens, RBAC — is on the customer. Encryption at rest is a tool, not a transfer of responsibility."},{id:"sr-6",prompt:"As you move from IaaS to PaaS to SaaS, what happens to the shared responsibility line?",options:["Microsoft takes on more of the stack; you still own data and access","You take on more of the physical datacenter","All responsibility moves to Microsoft, including your data","The model only applies to public cloud, not Azure"],answer:0,explanation:"Each step up the as-a-service ladder, Microsoft manages more (OS, then application). Data, identities, and access remain yours in every model."},{id:"sr-7",prompt:"Which responsibilities are ALWAYS the customer's, no matter which service type they use?",options:["Physical hosts, network, and datacenter","Information and data, devices (mobile and PCs), and accounts and identities","The hypervisor and the host operating system","Power, cooling, and building security"],answer:1,explanation:"Data, endpoints (devices), and accounts/identities stay with the customer in IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS. The physical layer always stays with Microsoft."},{id:"sr-8",prompt:"A company moves from running SQL Server on an Azure VM to Azure SQL Database. Which responsibility moves from the customer to Microsoft?",options:["Deciding who can query the data","Patching the database engine and the operating system underneath","Classifying sensitive columns","Choosing strong passwords for admin accounts"],answer:1,explanation:"Going from IaaS to PaaS hands OS and engine patching to Microsoft. Access, classification, and credentials stay with the customer."},{id:"sr-9",prompt:"In an on-premises datacenter, who is responsible for physical security of the servers?",options:["Microsoft","The customer","Shared equally by law","The internet provider"],answer:1,explanation:"On-premises, the customer owns everything, including the building and the locks. The shared responsibility model only shifts duties once you use a cloud provider."},{id:"sr-10",prompt:"A user's Microsoft 365 password is phished and an attacker reads their mailbox. Under the shared responsibility model, whose area was breached?",options:["Microsoft's — SaaS means Microsoft owns everything","The customer's — accounts and identities are always customer responsibility","Nobody's — SaaS has no responsibilities","The datacenter's physical security team"],answer:1,explanation:"Accounts and identities stay with the customer even in SaaS. Turning on MFA and training users is on the customer.",trap:"SaaS moves the most work to Microsoft, but never your accounts or your data."}]},{slug:"cloud-models",order:3,title:"Public, private, and hybrid cloud",emoji:"🌐",domain:"cloud",minutes:14,summary:"Public is Microsoft's shared Azure. Private is a cloud for one organization. Hybrid is both, connected on purpose. Multi-cloud exists, but AZ-900 grades you on the first three.",examFocus:"Read the constraint. Exclusive hardware or total control → private. Want Microsoft to own the fleet and a credit card to scale → public. Must keep some systems on-premises and still use Azure → hybrid.",youWillLearn:["What public, private, and hybrid actually mean","When each model is the right answer",'Why "we have a firewall" is not a private cloud',"Where multi-cloud fits — and why it is extra context, not the exam core"],officialModule:{...o.cloudComputing,units:["Define cloud models"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🏠",text:"Cloud models answer one question: whose datacenter, and who else is in it? A hotel (public), a house you own (private), or living in your house and booking a hotel for guests when the in-laws visit (hybrid). Same idea as IaaS/PaaS/SaaS, different axis — this axis is deployment, not how much of the stack Microsoft manages."},{type:"p",text:"AZ-900 wants three models: public, private, and hybrid. If a question mentions AWS plus Azure, that is multi-cloud — useful in real life, rarely the thing Microsoft is scoring. Start with the three, then we will park multi-cloud in a side note so it does not steal the answer."},{type:"think",text:"Public = shared hotel. Private = your house. Hybrid = house plus hotel on purpose."},{type:"heading",text:"Public cloud"},{type:"p",text:'Public cloud is what most people mean when they say "Azure." Microsoft owns the hardware, the buildings, and the global network. Anyone with a subscription can rent capacity. You share the physical fleet with other customers, but your resources are isolated logically. You get the catalog, the regions, and the ability to grow without buying a rack.'},{type:"example",title:"Harbor Coffee goes public",body:"Harbor Coffee is a 40-person company. They do not want a server room. They create an Azure subscription, put the ordering site on App Service, and store receipts in Blob Storage. Microsoft's datacenter, Microsoft's hardware, Harbor's data and access. That is public cloud."},{type:"list",items:["No CapEx for datacenter hardware — you rent","Fast to start, easy to scale globally","You share physical infrastructure with other tenants (logical isolation, not a shared login)","Least operational overhead for the building itself","You still pick regions, configure services, and own your data"]},{type:"heading",text:"Private cloud"},{type:"p",text:"Private cloud looks and feels like cloud — on-demand VMs, self-service, metering — but the hardware is dedicated to one organization. It might sit in your building or in a facility only you use. You (or a partner who runs it just for you) own the exclusive use of those hosts. Azure Stack Hub is Microsoft's way to run Azure-like services in your datacenter. The exam care-about is exclusive use and control, not the product name."},{type:"example",title:"A bank that cannot share a host",body:'A regulator tells Fabrikam Bank that a trading system must run on hardware no other company can touch, inside a building Fabrikam controls. Fabrikam still wants self-service VMs for developers. That is a private cloud use case — not because they are "serious," but because they need dedicated infrastructure.'},{type:"trap",text:'A single VM in Azure is not a private cloud. "Private" is about dedicated infrastructure for one tenant, not about a private IP or a network security group. Those are just good public-cloud hygiene.'},{type:"heading",text:"Hybrid cloud"},{type:"p",text:"Hybrid is the combination you meant to have: some workloads in a private environment (often on-premises), some in public Azure, connected so they work as one. Identity can sync. A database can stay in the basement while the website sits in Azure. Bursting — keep the baseline on-premises, overflow to Azure when a sale hits — is a classic hybrid story. Azure Arc, VPN Gateway, and ExpressRoute are the plumbing; you do not need those product deep-dives yet, just the idea that hybrid is connected on purpose."},{type:"diagram",title:"Three pictures",ascii:`PUBLIC                         PRIVATE
  Azure ☁️                       Your datacenter
  shared hardware                dedicated hardware
  many customers                 one organization

           HYBRID
   On-premises  <=====>  Azure
   (private-ish)         (public)
         one designed solution`},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Public",subtitle:"Azure, AWS, Google Cloud",points:["Provider owns the hardware","Best for most startups and scale-out apps","Lowest barrier to entry"]},{title:"Private",subtitle:"Your cloud, your hosts",points:["Dedicated hardware for one org","Control and isolation that some regulations demand","You still pay to run a datacenter"]},{title:"Hybrid",subtitle:"Both, connected",points:["Keep sensitive or awkward systems where they are","Use Azure for scale, DR, or new apps","Most large enterprises live here"]}]},{type:"heading",text:"Which model? Read the use case"},{type:"steps",items:[{title:"Public",text:'"We want to start fast, pay as we go, and let Microsoft run the datacenter." Seasonal scale. Global reach. No appetite to own racks.'},{title:"Private",text:'"This system must stay on dedicated hardware we control." Strict isolation, legacy licensing that forbids multi-tenant hosts, or a policy that data cannot leave our building.'},{title:"Hybrid",text:'"Some systems stay in our datacenter, and we also want Azure." Migration in progress. Low-latency factory machines on-site plus a cloud app. Burst to the public cloud. Backup/DR into Azure.'}]},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["Public: shared infrastructure, provider-owned, internet-delivered","Private: dedicated to one organization","Hybrid: public + private (or on-premises) together","Match the constraint in the story, not the company size"]},{type:"p",text:"Multi-cloud is using more than one public cloud — Azure plus AWS, for example. Companies do it to avoid lock-in or because a team already built something on another provider. Azure Arc can help you manage those resources from Azure. For AZ-900, multi-cloud is extra vocabulary. If the options are public / private / hybrid, pick among those three. Multi-cloud is not a fourth official model on the skills list."},{type:"trap",text:'Hybrid is not "we might use Azure someday." Hybrid is an architecture that uses both. And hybrid is not the same as multi-cloud. Hybrid = on-premises/private plus public. Multi-cloud = two public clouds.'},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Public cloud",what:"Provider-owned, shared hardware, you rent",remember:"Azure as most people use it"},{term:"Private cloud",what:"Cloud-style services on dedicated hardware",remember:"One tenant owns exclusive use"},{term:"Hybrid cloud",what:"Private/on-premises plus public, connected",remember:"Keep some, burst some, migrate some"},{term:"Multi-cloud",what:"More than one public provider",remember:"Extra context, not the AZ-900 trio"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Public is Microsoft's shared Azure, private is a cloud dedicated to one organization, and hybrid is using both together on purpose — pick the model that matches the constraint, not the buzzword."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"Tailwind Traders has a factory control system that must stay next to the machines, and a customer website they want to host in Azure and scale worldwide. They will connect the two with a private link.",answer:"Hybrid cloud",why:"Two environments, both in active use, connected. The factory is not moving. The website is public cloud. That combination is hybrid, not private-only and not public-only."}],quiz:[{id:"cm-1",prompt:"Which statement best describes the public cloud?",options:["Hardware dedicated to one company, usually in that company's building","Computing services on hardware the provider owns and many customers share","Using Azure and AWS at the same time","A VPN between two offices with no cloud provider"],answer:1,explanation:"Public cloud is the provider's shared infrastructure. Azure commercial regions are public cloud. Dedicated hardware is private. Two public providers is multi-cloud."},{id:"cm-2",prompt:"A regulator requires that a workload run on hardware no other customer can use, in a facility the company controls. Which model fits?",options:["Public cloud only","Private cloud","Spot VMs","Serverless"],answer:1,explanation:"Exclusive, dedicated infrastructure is the private-cloud cue. Public cloud shares physical hosts among customers (with logical isolation). Spot and serverless are pricing/compute styles, not deployment models."},{id:"cm-3",prompt:"An organization keeps its main database on-premises and runs the web front end in Azure, connected over ExpressRoute. What is that?",options:["Public cloud only","Private cloud only","Hybrid cloud","CapEx with no cloud"],answer:2,explanation:"On-premises plus Azure, working together, is hybrid. Public-only would mean nothing left in their datacenter. Private-only would mean no public Azure."},{id:"cm-4",prompt:"Why might a company choose public cloud over private cloud?",options:["They need hardware that no other company can physically share","They want to avoid owning and operating datacenter hardware and to scale quickly","They refuse to ever connect to the internet","Public cloud is the only model that can store data"],answer:1,explanation:"Public cloud trades exclusive hardware for speed, a huge catalog, and no CapEx on racks. If exclusive hardware is the requirement, that points to private, not public."},{id:"cm-5",prompt:'A team says they have a "private cloud" because their Azure VM has a private IP and an NSG. Are they using the AZ-900 meaning of private cloud?',options:["Yes — private IP means private cloud","Yes — NSGs convert public Azure into private cloud","No — a locked-down VM in Azure is still public cloud","No — private cloud only exists on AWS"],answer:2,explanation:"Private IP and NSGs are network controls inside public Azure. Private cloud means dedicated infrastructure for one organization. This is a favorite mix-up.",trap:"Private endpoint, private IP, and private cloud are different ideas. Only the last one is a deployment model."},{id:"cm-6",prompt:"Using both Microsoft Azure and Amazon Web Services for different apps is best described as:",options:["Private cloud","Hybrid cloud","Multi-cloud (extra context — not the main AZ-900 trio)","Serverless"],answer:2,explanation:"Two public clouds is multi-cloud. Hybrid is public plus private/on-premises. The exam's scored models are public, private, and hybrid; multi-cloud is useful vocabulary so you do not pick hybrid by mistake."},{id:"cm-7",prompt:"Which cloud model gives the MOST control over the hardware, but also requires the company to buy and maintain it?",options:["Public cloud","Private cloud","Hybrid cloud","Serverless"],answer:1,explanation:"Private cloud means dedicated infrastructure you control — including the cost and maintenance. Public cloud trades that control for no hardware to buy."},{id:"cm-8",prompt:"A retailer runs its stores' point-of-sale servers on-premises but bursts its website into Azure during the holiday season. Which model is this?",options:["Private cloud","Public cloud only","Hybrid cloud","Multi-cloud"],answer:2,explanation:"On-premises plus public cloud, working together, is hybrid. Cloud bursting is a classic hybrid use case."},{id:"cm-9",prompt:"Which Azure service helps manage on-premises and multi-cloud servers as if they were Azure resources?",options:["Azure Arc","Azure DNS","Azure Advisor","Azure Blob Storage"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure Arc projects servers, Kubernetes clusters, and data services from anywhere into Azure Resource Manager so you can manage them with Azure tools."},{id:"cm-10",prompt:"Which is a typical characteristic of the public cloud?",options:["No CapEx, and you can scale quickly with pay-as-you-go pricing","The organization owns the physical servers","Only one customer ever uses the provider's hardware","It cannot be connected to on-premises networks"],answer:0,explanation:"Public cloud: no hardware to buy, fast scaling, pay for what you use. The provider owns the hardware and many customers share it."}]},{slug:"cloud-benefits",order:4,title:"Benefits of using cloud services",emoji:"📈",domain:"cloud",minutes:16,summary:"High availability, scale-up vs scale-out, elasticity, reliability, cost and performance predictability, security, governance, and manageability — the reasons companies actually move.",examFocus:"Microsoft will describe a symptom (Black Friday traffic, a failed disk, a surprise bill, a patch Tuesday) and want the benefit by name. Vertical vs horizontal scaling is the mix-up they never get tired of.",youWillLearn:["High availability vs reliability, in human language","Vertical scaling, horizontal scaling, and elasticity","Performance predictability vs cost predictability","What security, governance, and manageability mean in the cloud","Why sustainability is now on the list of cloud benefits"],officialModule:{...o.cloudBenefits,units:["Describe the benefits of high availability and scalability in the cloud","Describe the benefits of reliability and predictability in the cloud","Describe the benefits of security and governance in the cloud","Describe the benefits of manageability in the cloud","Describe sustainability considerations in the cloud"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🎯",text:'Companies do not move to Azure because "cloud" sounds modern. They move because a well-designed cloud app can stay up, grow when it is busy, shrink when it is quiet, fail over when hardware dies, and be managed without a plane ticket to a datacenter. This lesson is the vocabulary for those wins.'},{type:"p",text:'AZ-900 groups the benefits into four clusters: high availability and scalability; reliability and predictability; security and governance; manageability. Learn the clusters, then learn the words inside them so a scenario maps to one term, not a vague "it\'s better."'},{type:"think",text:'Every benefit answers a pain: downtime, sudden load, surprise bills, patch chaos, or "who is allowed to do what."'},{type:"heading",text:"High availability"},{type:"p",text:"Availability is the percentage of time a system does what users need. High availability (HA) means you designed it so a single failure does not take the whole thing down — extra instances, extra datacenters, load balancers, SLAs. Azure regions and availability zones exist so you can spread risk. An SLA like 99.99% is a contract about that design, not a magic spell."},{type:"example",title:"One oven vs two kitchens",body:"A restaurant with one oven is cheap until the oven dies on Saturday night. Two kitchens on two streets is high availability. In Azure that might be two VMs in two availability zones behind a load balancer, not a bigger single VM."},{type:"heading",text:"Scalability: up vs out"},{type:"p",text:"Scalability is the ability to increase (or decrease) capacity when load changes. There are two directions, and the exam will use both names."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Vertical scaling",subtitle:"Scale up / scale down",points:["Same machine, more (or less) CPU, RAM, or disk","Like replacing a laptop with a workstation","Simple, but you eventually hit a ceiling","Often involves a resize (and sometimes a reboot)"]},{title:"Horizontal scaling",subtitle:"Scale out / scale in",points:["More machines (or fewer), same role","Like opening more checkout lanes","The cloud-native way to handle huge load","Needs a load balancer and an app that can run in many copies"]}]},{type:"diagram",title:"Up is bigger. Out is more.",ascii:`VERTICAL (scale up)              HORIZONTAL (scale out)

   [  small VM  ]                  [ VM ] [ VM ]
          ↓                        [ VM ] [ VM ] [ VM ]
   [   BIG VM   ]                     more copies

  one box, more power             more boxes, same job`},{type:"p",text:"Elasticity is scalability with an automatic spring. The system adds capacity when load rises and removes it when load falls, without a human opening a ticket. Black Friday is the story: scale out at 6 p.m., scale in at 2 a.m., stop paying for idle VMs. Scalability says you can grow. Elasticity says you grow and shrink by itself."},{type:"think",text:"Scale up = bigger box. Scale out = more boxes. Elasticity = the boxes appear and vanish on their own."},{type:"trap",text:'High availability is not the same as scalability. HA is "still up when something breaks." Scale is "handles more work." A single huge VM can be scalable vertically and still be a terrible HA story — that one VM is a single point of failure.'},{type:"heading",text:"Reliability and predictability"},{type:"p",text:"Reliability is the system's ability to recover from failures and keep providing a consistent service — resiliency plus availability. Disaster recovery is the big-failure cousin: restore within an acceptable time (RTO) with an acceptable amount of data loss (RPO). The cloud helps because you can copy workloads to another region without buying a second building first."},{type:"p",text:"Predictability has two flavors on AZ-900, and they are not interchangeable. Performance predictability means the app behaves consistently — you can size, test, and autoscale so users do not see random slowness. Cost predictability means you can forecast the bill — budgets, reservations, alerts — instead of discovering a surprise invoice. Same word, two exam answers."},{type:"example",title:"Northwind wants both kinds of predictable",body:'Finance wants a forecast within 10% each month (cost predictability) so they buy reservations for the always-on VMs. The website team wants checkout to stay under 200 ms even at noon (performance predictability) so they load-test and set autoscale rules. Both are "predictability." The scenario tells you which one.'},{type:"heading",text:"Security and governance"},{type:"p",text:'Security in the cloud is not "Microsoft already did it" — you saw that in shared responsibility. The benefit is that you inherit a huge physical security program, plus a catalog of tools (identity, encryption, network controls, threat detection) that a 20-person company could not build alone. You still configure them.'},{type:"p",text:"Governance is how the organization stays in control: policies, standards, who can create what, which regions are allowed, tags for cost owners, audits. In Azure that later becomes Azure Policy, management groups, and RBAC. For this lesson, remember the idea: cloud makes it easier to apply the same rules everywhere, instead of hoping each office's server closet matches the spreadsheet."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["HA: design so failures do not take you fully down","Vertical = bigger VM; horizontal = more VMs","Elasticity = automatic scale out and in","Reliability: recover and keep serving","Predictability: performance and/or cost","Security: platform capabilities + your configuration","Governance: enforce organizational rules at scale"]},{type:"heading",text:"Manageability in the cloud"},{type:"p",text:"Manageability is the unglamorous superpower. You manage resources through a browser (Azure portal), command lines (CLI, PowerShell, Cloud Shell), APIs, and templates — from anywhere, without a crash cart. Microsoft can patch the platform underneath PaaS and SaaS. You can automate the boring work. A related word you will see is agility: the ability to stand up a new environment in minutes when the business changes its mind."},{type:"diagram",title:"Benefits as a map",ascii:`Stay up ─────────── High availability
Handle more ─────── Scalability (up or out)
Auto spring ─────── Elasticity
Get back up ─────── Reliability / DR
No surprises ────── Predictability (perf + cost)
Stay safe ───────── Security
Stay in control ─── Governance
Run it without flying to a rack ── Manageability`},{type:"heading",text:"Sustainability"},{type:"p",text:"The newest benefit on the Microsoft Learn list is sustainability. Large cloud datacenters run hardware much more efficiently than a server closet: servers are shared by many customers, so fewer machines sit idle; cooling and power are engineered at huge scale; and Microsoft buys renewable energy for its regions. When you scale in at night or delete what you do not use, you also stop drawing power for it. Microsoft gives customers carbon-emissions reporting so they can see the footprint of their cloud usage."},{type:"think",text:"Sustainability = do the same work with less energy and less idle hardware. Right-sizing and elasticity save money AND carbon."},{type:"trap",text:"Sustainability does not mean “Azure is carbon-free by default” or “you no longer have responsibilities.” You still choose sizes, regions, and when to turn things off. Unused resources still burn energy and money."},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Sustainability",what:"Efficient shared datacenters, renewable energy, emissions reporting",remember:"Turn off what you do not use"},{term:"High availability",what:"Keep serving when parts fail",remember:"Extra instances, zones, SLAs"},{term:"Vertical scaling",what:"Bigger (or smaller) machine",remember:"Scale up / down"},{term:"Horizontal scaling",what:"More (or fewer) machines",remember:"Scale out / in"},{term:"Elasticity",what:"Automatic scale with demand",remember:"The spring, not just the ability"},{term:"Reliability",what:"Recover and stay consistent",remember:"Resiliency + availability"},{term:"Predictability",what:"Stable performance and/or forecastable cost",remember:"Read which one the story wants"},{term:"Governance",what:"Org policies applied to cloud resources",remember:"Control, not just security tools"},{term:"Manageability",what:"Operate via portal, CLI, APIs, automation",remember:"No plane ticket to the rack"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Cloud benefits are staying up (HA), growing the right way (scale up vs out, elasticity), recovering (reliability), avoiding surprises (predictable performance and cost), and running the place with security, governance, and remote manageability."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"PixelForge's game store dies every launch day because one web server maxes out. They can tolerate many identical servers behind a load balancer, and they want extra servers to appear only during launches, then disappear.",answer:"Horizontal scaling plus elasticity (scale out automatically, then scale in)",why:'A bigger single server would be vertical scaling and still one point of failure. The "appear and disappear" language is elasticity, not a one-time resize.'}],quiz:[{id:"cb-1",prompt:"A team resizes a virtual machine from 4 vCPUs to 16 vCPUs. What is that?",options:["Horizontal scaling (scale out)","Vertical scaling (scale up)","A new availability zone","Governance"],answer:1,explanation:"Same resource, more power = vertical / scale up. Horizontal would add more VMs."},{id:"cb-2",prompt:"An online shop automatically adds web servers when CPU is high and removes them at night. Which benefit is that primarily?",options:["CapEx","Elasticity","Private cloud","Spot eviction"],answer:1,explanation:"Automatic grow-and-shrink with demand is elasticity. Scalability is the broader ability to grow; elasticity is the automatic spring."},{id:"cb-3",prompt:"A company deploys two VMs in two availability zones behind a load balancer so a datacenter issue does not take the site down. Which benefit is the main goal?",options:["High availability","Vertical scaling","Cost CapEx","Multi-cloud"],answer:0,explanation:'Spreading instances so a failure does not kill the service is high availability. Vertical scaling would have been "make the one VM bigger."',trap:"Do not call this elasticity unless the story also says instances appear and vanish automatically with load."},{id:"cb-4",prompt:"Finance wants next quarter's Azure bill to be forecastable within a range, using reservations and budgets. Which benefit is that?",options:["Performance predictability","Cost predictability","Horizontal scaling","Physical datacenter ownership"],answer:1,explanation:"Predictability splits into performance and cost. Budgets and reservations are cost. Load tests and autoscale for latency are performance."},{id:"cb-5",prompt:"Which statement best describes governance in the cloud?",options:["Microsoft physically guards the datacenter so you need no policies","Applying organizational rules and standards to cloud resources","Always choosing the largest VM size","Deleting all logs to save money"],answer:1,explanation:"Governance is control: policies, allowed regions, naming, tags, who can deploy. Physical guards are Microsoft's security of the building, which is not the same thing."},{id:"cb-6",prompt:"An admin in another country updates a resource group from Cloud Shell in a browser. Which benefit is that illustrating?",options:["Manageability","Spot pricing","Private cloud hardware exclusive use","Vertical scaling"],answer:0,explanation:"Manageability is operating the environment remotely through portal, CLI, APIs, and automation — without standing in the datacenter."},{id:"cb-7",prompt:"Which statement describes how the cloud can support sustainability goals?",options:["Shared, efficiently run datacenters mean less idle hardware and energy per workload","Cloud resources use no electricity","Microsoft guarantees every customer is carbon-neutral automatically","Sustainability only applies to private clouds"],answer:0,explanation:"High utilization of shared hardware, efficient cooling, and renewable energy purchases reduce the footprint per workload. Customers still choose what to run and when."},{id:"cb-8",prompt:"A web app recovers automatically when a VM fails and keeps serving users. Which benefit is described?",options:["Reliability","Cost predictability","Governance","Vertical scaling"],answer:0,explanation:"Reliability is the ability to recover from failures and keep functioning. HA is closely related; the recovery wording points to reliability."},{id:"cb-9",prompt:"A company wants to use templates and policies so every new environment is deployed the same way and stays compliant. Which benefit pair is this?",options:["Governance and manageability","Elasticity and Spot pricing","High availability and region pairs","CapEx and depreciation"],answer:0,explanation:"Standard templates and policies are governance; deploying and managing them through automation is manageability."},{id:"cb-10",prompt:"Which phrase describes scaling OUT?",options:["Adding more instances of a resource","Adding more CPU to a single instance","Moving the app to another region","Reducing the price with a reservation"],answer:0,explanation:"Scale out = more instances (horizontal). Scale up = more power per instance (vertical)."}]},{slug:"cloud-service-types",order:5,title:"IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS",emoji:"🍽️",domain:"cloud",minutes:18,summary:"Infrastructure, platform, and software as a service — who manages what, when to pick each, and the classic VM vs App Service vs Microsoft 365 comparison.",examFocus:"If you must manage the operating system, it is IaaS. If you deploy code and Microsoft runs the OS, it is PaaS. If you just use the app in a browser, it is SaaS. Watch for Azure Virtual Machines vs Azure App Service vs Microsoft 365 / Outlook.",youWillLearn:["What IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS each give you","Who patches what in each model","When a VM, App Service, or Microsoft 365 is the right picture","How to read exam scenarios without mixing the three"],officialModule:{...o.cloudServiceTypes,units:["Describe Infrastructure as a Service","Describe Platform as a Service","Describe Software as a Service"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🍕",text:'IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS are not three brands of Azure. They are three levels of "how much dinner Microsoft cooks." IaaS rents you a kitchen. PaaS rents you a kitchen that is already set up — you bring the recipe (your code). SaaS is a restaurant: you sit down and eat. The exam is almost always asking which level of control — and which level of chores — the story describes.'},{type:"p",text:"You already saw this ladder on the shared responsibility lesson. Here we stay on the services themselves: what you buy, what you still do, and the three examples Microsoft loves — virtual machines, App Service, and Microsoft 365 (Outlook, Teams, Word in the browser)."},{type:"think",text:"IaaS = rent the VM. PaaS = rent the platform, deploy your app. SaaS = use the finished software."},{type:"heading",text:"IaaS — infrastructure as a service"},{type:"p",text:'IaaS is the closest cloud cousin of "a server in the basement." Microsoft provides the virtual hardware: VMs, disks, virtual networks, load balancers. You choose the OS image, patch it, install IIS or nginx, configure backups, and own the guest. You gained speed (a VM in minutes) and skipped buying a rack. You did not skip being a sysadmin for that VM.'},{type:"list",items:["Azure Virtual Machines is the textbook IaaS example","Also in the IaaS neighborhood: Virtual Network, many load-balancer setups, when you own the OS","Use it to lift-and-shift an existing server, run custom software that needs the full OS, or lift a network appliance","Most control, most of the chores"]},{type:"example",title:"Contoso lifts a line-of-business VM",body:'Contoso has a 2016 Windows box that runs a vendor app which "must see a Windows server." They create an Azure VM, attach a disk, join it to a virtual network, and keep patching Windows. That is IaaS. They did not rewrite the app for App Service. They rented a server.'},{type:"heading",text:"PaaS — platform as a service"},{type:"p",text:"PaaS is the sweet spot for a lot of new apps. Microsoft provides the OS, runtime, and platform features (deployment slots, scaling, patches). You provide the application and its configuration. Azure App Service (web apps) is the classic. Azure SQL Database is PaaS for databases: you get a database, not a SQL VM you have to patch. Azure Functions (serverless) sits at the PaaS end of the spectrum — even less server to see."},{type:"example",title:"Fabrikam deploys a web API",body:'Fabrikam\'s developers push code to App Service. Nobody RDPs into Windows to install .NET. Microsoft keeps the platform current. Fabrikam still writes the API, stores secrets correctly, and decides who can call it. If the exam says "host a web app without managing the OS," it wants PaaS — usually App Service.'},{type:"think",text:"PaaS = you bring the app. Microsoft brings the OS and the plumbing."},{type:"heading",text:"SaaS — software as a service"},{type:"p",text:"SaaS is finished software delivered over the internet. You configure it and use it. You do not deploy code to it (except maybe extensions). Microsoft 365 — Outlook, Exchange Online, Teams, Word, SharePoint — is the AZ-900 example you should be able to pick in your sleep. Dynamics 365 is another. You are a tenant of the application. Microsoft runs the app, the OS, and the hardware."},{type:"example",title:"Harbor Coffee's email",body:"Harbor Coffee does not install Exchange Server. Staff open Outlook on the web or the Outlook app and send mail. That is SaaS. If they had built a VM, installed Exchange, and patched it, that would have been IaaS — and a much worse Monday."},{type:"diagram",title:"Who manages what",ascii:`                 IaaS          PaaS          SaaS
                 ----          ----          ----
Data             YOU           YOU           YOU
Access           YOU           YOU           YOU
Application      YOU           YOU*          Microsoft
Runtime          YOU           Microsoft     Microsoft
OS               YOU           Microsoft     Microsoft
Virtualization   Microsoft     Microsoft     Microsoft
Hardware         Microsoft     Microsoft     Microsoft

* Your code on PaaS; Microsoft's app on SaaS`},{type:"interactive",diagram:"shared-responsibility"},{type:"heading",text:"The comparison the exam will actually give you"},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Azure Virtual Machines",subtitle:"IaaS",points:["You pick and patch the OS","Lift-and-shift, custom software, full control","You are still the sysadmin of that guest"]},{title:"Azure App Service",subtitle:"PaaS",points:["You deploy a web app / API","Microsoft manages OS and web platform",'"Host a website without managing servers"']},{title:"Microsoft 365 / Outlook",subtitle:"SaaS",points:["You use email, docs, Teams","Microsoft runs the application",'"Give people email without installing Exchange"']}]},{type:"table",caption:"Pick the service type from the story",headers:["If the story says…","Think","Typical Azure example"],rows:[["Migrate a server as-is; need the OS; install extra agents","IaaS","Azure Virtual Machines"],["Deploy our code; do not manage Windows/Linux","PaaS","App Service, Azure SQL Database, Functions"],["Users just need the app; we will not build it","SaaS","Microsoft 365, Outlook, Teams, Dynamics 365"]]},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["IaaS: infrastructure, you manage the OS","PaaS: platform, you manage your app and data","SaaS: software, you manage data and access","VM vs App Service vs Microsoft 365 is the triangle to memorize"]},{type:"trap",text:"Azure SQL Database is PaaS. A SQL Server you install on a VM is IaaS. Same database engine family, different chore list. If you have to patch Windows or SQL Server yourself, you are in IaaS. If Azure SQL patches for you, you are in PaaS."},{type:"trap",text:'The Azure portal is how you manage Azure. It is not "SaaS vs IaaS" for your workload. Do not pick SaaS just because someone used a browser. Outlook is SaaS. Creating a VM in the portal is still IaaS.'},{type:"steps",items:[{title:'Hear "virtual machine" or "install on Windows Server"',text:"IaaS."},{title:'Hear "web app," "App Service," or "don\'t manage the OS"',text:"PaaS."},{title:'Hear "Outlook," "Microsoft 365," "Teams," or "just use the software"',text:"SaaS."},{title:'Hear "serverless" / Azure Functions',text:"Treat it as PaaS (the extreme end): you bring code, no servers to see."}]},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"IaaS",what:"Rent virtual hardware",remember:"You patch the OS — Azure VMs"},{term:"PaaS",what:"Rent a ready platform for your code",remember:"App Service, Azure SQL, Functions"},{term:"SaaS",what:"Rent finished software",remember:"Microsoft 365, Outlook, Teams"},{term:"Most management",what:"IaaS",remember:"Most control, most chores"},{term:"Least management",what:"SaaS",remember:"Least control of the app itself"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"IaaS rents you the VM and you run the OS, PaaS rents you a platform so you only run your app, and SaaS rents you the app itself — VMs, App Service, and Microsoft 365 are the three pictures to keep in your pocket."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"Adventure Works wants employees to have email, calendars, and Teams meetings. They do not want to install or patch Exchange Server. They also have a custom public website they will write in .NET and do not want to manage Windows for that site. Finally, a legacy payroll package must run on a specific Windows Server version.",answer:"Email/Teams = SaaS (Microsoft 365). Website = PaaS (App Service). Payroll = IaaS (Azure VM).",why:"One company, three service types. The exam loves stacking them in a single paragraph so you match each workload instead of picking one answer for the whole company."}],quiz:[{id:"cst-1",prompt:"Which Azure offering is the classic example of IaaS?",options:["Microsoft 365 Outlook","Azure Virtual Machines","Azure App Service","Microsoft Teams"],answer:1,explanation:"Virtual machines are IaaS: you manage the guest OS. App Service is PaaS. Outlook and Teams are SaaS."},{id:"cst-2",prompt:"A developer wants to host a web app in Azure without managing or patching the operating system. What should they use?",options:["An Azure virtual machine they RDP into","Azure App Service (PaaS)","Exchange Server on a VM","Buying a physical server for the office"],answer:1,explanation:'"Web app" + "no OS management" is App Service / PaaS. A VM you RDP into is IaaS and you still patch it.'},{id:"cst-3",prompt:"Employees use Outlook on the web for email. Which cloud service type is that?",options:["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","Private cloud only"],answer:2,explanation:"Outlook / Microsoft 365 is software as a service. You use the application; Microsoft runs it."},{id:"cst-4",prompt:"Who is responsible for the guest operating system on an Azure VM versus on Azure App Service?",options:["Microsoft for the VM, you for App Service","You for the VM (IaaS), Microsoft for App Service (PaaS)","You for both","Microsoft for both, including your application code"],answer:1,explanation:"IaaS: customer patches the guest OS. PaaS: Microsoft patches the platform OS. In both cases you still own your data and (on PaaS) your app code.",trap:"Do not say Microsoft patches your VM just because the VM lives in Azure."},{id:"cst-5",prompt:"A vendor app cannot be rewritten and must run on Windows Server with a specific MSI installer. Which service type fits?",options:["SaaS Microsoft 365","IaaS Azure Virtual Machines","Only a private cloud can run Windows","Spot pricing as a service type"],answer:1,explanation:"Full OS and custom installers are IaaS territory. Microsoft 365 will not run that MSI. Spot is a VM pricing option, not a service type."},{id:"cst-6",prompt:"Azure SQL Database (you get a database, Azure patches the engine) compared with SQL Server installed on a VM is a contrast between:",options:["SaaS vs public cloud","PaaS vs IaaS","Hybrid vs multi-cloud","CapEx vs a physical rack you still own in Azure"],answer:1,explanation:"Azure SQL Database is PaaS. SQL Server on a VM is IaaS. Same family of product, different management split — a favorite exam distinction."},{id:"cst-7",prompt:"Which scenario is a typical use case for IaaS?",options:["Lift-and-shift migration of existing servers to the cloud with minimal changes","Using a hosted email service","Building a new web app without managing any OS","Signing in to a CRM in a browser"],answer:0,explanation:"Lift-and-shift moves VMs largely as-is — IaaS. Hosted email and browser CRMs are SaaS. A new web app without an OS is PaaS."},{id:"cst-8",prompt:"A development team wants a managed environment with built-in tools, so they can focus on writing code rather than infrastructure. Which service type?",options:["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","On-premises"],answer:1,explanation:"PaaS provides the runtime, tooling, and managed infrastructure. Developers bring code and data."},{id:"cst-9",prompt:"Which service type gives the customer the LEAST management responsibility?",options:["IaaS","PaaS","SaaS","A private cloud"],answer:2,explanation:"SaaS is a finished application. The customer manages data, devices, and accounts — little else."},{id:"cst-10",prompt:"Which statement about IaaS is correct?",options:["It is the most flexible cloud service type and gives the most control over the OS","Microsoft patches the guest OS for you","You cannot choose your own operating system","It is the same as SaaS"],answer:0,explanation:"IaaS = maximum control and flexibility — and maximum customer responsibility among the three service types."}]},{slug:"regions-and-zones",order:6,title:"Regions, availability zones, and datacenters",emoji:"🌍",domain:"architecture",minutes:16,summary:"Where Azure actually lives: regions, region pairs, sovereign clouds, datacenters, and availability zones — and how to pick a place for your stuff.",examFocus:"Microsoft loves mixing region, region pair, and availability zone. If you can say what each one protects you from, the questions get easy.",youWillLearn:["What an Azure region is, and why companies pick one over another","How region pairs help with disaster recovery","What sovereign regions are for","The difference between a datacenter and an availability zone"],officialModule:{...o.coreArchitecture,units:["What is Microsoft Azure","Describe Azure physical infrastructure"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🗺️",text:"Azure is not one giant computer sitting in one building. It is a worldwide collection of buildings, grouped into regions. Once you can picture that map, availability zones and region pairs stop feeling like random vocabulary."},{type:"p",text:"Think of Azure like a global chain of warehouses. Each warehouse is a datacenter. Nearby warehouses are grouped into a city — that city is a region. Some cities have several independent warehouses so one fire does not take the whole city down. Those independent warehouses are availability zones."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Azure regions"},{type:"p",text:"An Azure region is a geographic area that contains one or more Azure datacenters. You choose a region when you create most resources. Examples you will see on the exam: East US, West Europe, Japan East, Australia East. The region is the location you pick. It is not a product you install."},{type:"example",title:"A region is like choosing a city for your shop",body:"If your customers are in London, putting the shop in West Europe usually feels faster than putting it in East US. The shop is still “Azure.” You just chose a closer city."},{type:"think",text:"Region = a geographic area with Azure datacenters. You pick one when you create a resource."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Why you pick a region"},{type:"p",text:"The exam expects you to know the practical reasons, not every region name. Latency: put resources close to the people who use them so round-trips stay short. Data residency: some laws say customer data must stay in a country or a union, so you pick a region that keeps it there. Service availability: not every Azure service exists in every region, and some features arrive in a few regions first. Cost can also differ by region, but AZ-900 leans hardest on latency, residency, and whether the service you need is actually offered there."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam point",points:["Latency — closer region, snappier app","Data residency / compliance — data stays where the law requires","Service availability — the feature or SKU must exist in that region","A region is a location choice, not a high-availability feature by itself"]},{type:"heading",text:"3. Region pairs"},{type:"p",text:"Most Azure regions are paired with another region in the same geography, usually hundreds of miles apart. Microsoft treats that pair as a disaster-recovery partnership. Platform updates are rolled out to one region in the pair first, then the other, so a bad update is less likely to hit both at once. If a whole region has a major outage, Microsoft prioritizes recovery of the pair. Geo-redundant storage copies data to the paired region for you."},{type:"diagram",title:"A region pair, same geography, far apart",ascii:`  Geography: United States
  ┌──────────────┐          ┌──────────────┐
  │   East US    │  pair    │   West US    │
  │  (primary)   │ <------> │  (secondary) │
  └──────────────┘  ~far    └──────────────┘
         If East US goes dark, West US is the planned fallback.`},{type:"trap",text:"A region pair is not the same as an availability zone. Pairs protect you when a whole region fails. Zones protect you when one datacenter inside a region fails."},{type:"heading",text:"4. Sovereign regions"},{type:"p",text:"Sovereign regions are isolated Azure clouds for governments and countries that need stricter isolation. They are not just “another region on the public map.” Azure Government is a separate cloud for US government customers. Azure China is operated by 21Vianet, physically in China, with its own portal and identities. You may also see Azure Germany in exam material as an example of a sovereign / specialized cloud. The idea to remember: compliance and isolation, not cheaper VMs."},{type:"example",title:"Public Azure vs a sovereign cloud",body:"A startup in Texas uses public Azure (East US). A US federal agency that must keep data in a government-only cloud uses Azure Government. Same kinds of services, different isolated environment."},{type:"think",text:"Sovereign region = isolated Azure cloud for legal / government isolation. Not a fancy availability zone."},{type:"heading",text:"5. Azure datacenters"},{type:"p",text:"A datacenter is the physical building: racks of servers, storage, networking, power, and cooling. You do not pick a datacenter by name when you create a VM. You pick a region (and, if the region supports it, an availability zone). Microsoft places your resource onto hardware inside one of those buildings. Several datacenters together make up a region. When an availability zone exists, it is typically one or more datacenters that Microsoft treats as one failure boundary."},{type:"diagram",title:"Buildings inside a region",ascii:`              Azure region  (example: East US)
     ┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
     │  Datacenter A    Datacenter B    DC C   │
     │  [servers]       [servers]      [servers]│
     └─────────────────────────────────────────┘
     You choose the region. Azure chooses the building
     unless you also choose an availability zone.`},{type:"heading",text:"6. Availability zones"},{type:"p",text:"An availability zone (AZ) is a physically separate location inside a region. Each zone has its own power, cooling, and networking. A typical region that supports zones has at least three. If you spread two or more VMs across zones, a fire or power failure in one building should not take all of them down. That is how Azure can offer a higher VM SLA when you use zones. Not every region has availability zones, and not every service is zone-redundant yet — another reason region choice matters."},{type:"diagram",title:"Zones are separate buildings in one region",ascii:`                 East US region
        ┌──────────┬──────────┬──────────┐
        │  Zone 1  │  Zone 2  │  Zone 3  │
        │  (DC)    │  (DC)    │  (DC)    │
        │  VM-A    │  VM-B    │  VM-C    │
        └──────────┴──────────┴──────────┘
        Independent power, cooling, and network.
        One zone can fail. The others can keep running.`},{type:"trap",text:"Availability zones are inside one region. They do not replace a region pair. Zones = datacenter failure. Pair = regional disaster."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Availability zone",subtitle:"Inside a region",points:["Separate datacenter with its own power and cooling","Protects against a building-level failure","Used for high availability of production apps"]},{title:"Region pair",subtitle:"Across two regions",points:["Two regions in the same geography, far apart","Protects against a whole-region disaster","Used for disaster recovery and geo-redundant storage"]}]},{type:"interactive",diagram:"regions-zones"},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Datacenter",what:"Physical building with servers",remember:"The warehouse"},{term:"Availability zone",what:"Independent datacenter(s) in a region",remember:"Survive one building failing"},{term:"Region",what:"Geographic area you select for resources",remember:"The city on the map"},{term:"Region pair",what:"Two regions Microsoft pairs for DR",remember:"Survive a whole city failing"},{term:"Sovereign region",what:"Isolated Azure cloud for compliance",remember:"Government / China-style isolation"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"A datacenter is a building, availability zones are isolated buildings inside a region, a region is the geographic area you choose for latency, residency, and service availability, region pairs help when a whole region fails, and sovereign regions are isolated clouds for legal isolation."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"An online shop must keep European customer data in the EU, keep pages fast for shoppers in France, and stay up if one Azure datacenter loses power.",answer:"Deploy in a West Europe (or other EU) region that has availability zones, and spread the app across zones.",why:"The EU region handles residency and latency. Availability zones handle a single datacenter failure. A US region would break residency even if it were cheaper."}],quiz:[{id:"rz-1",prompt:"What is an Azure region?",options:["A single physical server rack Microsoft rents to you","A geographic area that contains one or more Azure datacenters","A paired backup copy of a storage account","A physically separate building with its own power inside one city"],answer:1,explanation:"A region is a geographic location made of one or more datacenters. You select it when you create most resources.",trap:"The last option describes an availability zone, not a region."},{id:"rz-2",prompt:"A company needs the app to keep running if one Azure datacenter in the same region catches fire. What should they use?",options:["A sovereign region","A region pair only, with everything in one datacenter","Availability zones in a region that supports them","A different Azure subscription"],answer:2,explanation:"Availability zones are separate datacenters inside a region with independent power, cooling, and networking.",trap:"A region pair helps when an entire region fails, which is a bigger disaster than one building."},{id:"rz-3",prompt:"What is the main purpose of an Azure region pair?",options:["To give every VM a public IP address","To provide a Microsoft-planned partner region for disaster recovery","To isolate US government workloads from public Azure","To split a virtual network into subnets"],answer:1,explanation:"Most regions are paired with another region in the same geography so platform updates and regional recovery have a planned partner."},{id:"rz-4",prompt:"A US federal agency must run workloads in an isolated Azure environment that is not the public cloud. Which option matches that need?",options:["Any availability zone in East US","A resource group named Government","Azure Government (a sovereign cloud)","A region pair between East US and West US"],answer:2,explanation:"Sovereign regions such as Azure Government are isolated clouds for legal and government isolation, not just another zone in public Azure."},{id:"rz-5",prompt:"Which is a real reason to choose one Azure region over another?",options:["Regions determine your Microsoft Entra tenant name","Every region has identical services, prices, and latency","Latency to users, data residency rules, and whether a service is available there","Only region pairs can host virtual machines"],answer:2,explanation:"You pick a region for closeness to users, legal data-residency needs, and service availability. Not every service ships in every region."},{id:"rz-6",prompt:"How does an availability zone relate to a datacenter?",options:["A zone is a logical folder; it is not a physical place","A zone is typically one or more datacenters in a region with independent power, cooling, and networking","A zone always spans two paired regions","A zone is Microsoft’s name for a sovereign cloud"],answer:1,explanation:"Availability zones are physical isolation inside a region. Each zone is made of datacenter facilities that can fail independently."},{id:"rz-7",prompt:"What is the minimum number of availability zones in a region that supports them?",options:["One","Two","Three","Ten"],answer:2,explanation:"Zone-enabled regions have a minimum of three separate availability zones, each with independent power, cooling, and networking."},{id:"rz-8",prompt:"Which statement about region pairs is correct?",options:["Planned Azure updates roll out to one region of a pair at a time","Paired regions are always in different countries","Every region pair is in the same datacenter","Region pairs replace the need for availability zones"],answer:0,explanation:"Microsoft staggers platform updates across a pair and prioritizes recovering one region of the pair during a broad outage. Most pairs are in the same geography (often the same country) for data residency.",trap:"Pairs are usually at least 300 miles apart but within the same geography — not “always different countries.”"},{id:"rz-9",prompt:"Azure China 21Vianet is an example of what?",options:["A sovereign region","An availability zone","A region pair","A management group"],answer:0,explanation:"Sovereign regions are isolated instances of Azure for legal or compliance reasons — Azure Government (US) and Azure operated by 21Vianet (China)."},{id:"rz-10",prompt:"Which Azure services are deployed across zones automatically by the platform, so you do not choose a zone?",options:["Zone-redundant services","Zonal services","Non-regional services","Sovereign services"],answer:0,explanation:"Zonal services are pinned to one zone you pick. Zone-redundant services replicate across zones automatically. Non-regional services (like Microsoft Entra ID) are always available across geographies."}]},{slug:"resource-hierarchy",order:7,title:"The Azure resource hierarchy",emoji:"🗂️",domain:"architecture",minutes:15,summary:"Management groups, subscriptions, resource groups, and resources — who bills, who organizes, and where policy and RBAC hang.",examFocus:"If a question mentions billing, think subscription. If it mentions a logical folder for resources you create, think resource group. If it mentions applying policy to many subscriptions at once, think management group.",youWillLearn:["What a resource and a resource group actually are","Why a subscription is the billing (and quota) boundary","What management groups sit above subscriptions to do","How RBAC and policy flow down the hierarchy","Which Azure account types exist for getting started"],officialModule:{...o.coreArchitecture,units:["Get started with Azure accounts","Describe Azure management infrastructure"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"📁",text:"Azure organizes everything in a tree. Once you can draw that tree from memory, a surprising number of AZ-900 questions are just “which level of the tree is this talking about?”"},{type:"diagram",title:"The hierarchy you must be able to sketch",ascii:`  Management groups
          │
          ▼
     Subscriptions     ← billing & quotas
          │
          ▼
    Resource groups    ← organize & lifecycle
          │
          ▼
       Resources       ← the actual stuff (VM, disk, VNet…)`},{type:"heading",text:"1. Resources"},{type:"p",text:"A resource is anything you create in Azure: a virtual machine, a disk, a virtual network, a storage account, an App Service plan. Each resource has a type, a name, and a region. You do not create “Azure” itself — you create resources inside a subscription."},{type:"think",text:"Resource = one thing you deploy. If you can see it in the portal and it has a billable meter, it is probably a resource."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Resource groups"},{type:"p",text:"A resource group (RG) is a logical container for resources. It is not a datacenter and it is not a network. You use it to group things that share a lifecycle — for example, all the pieces of “the HR app in production.” Delete the resource group, and Azure deletes the resources in it. A resource belongs to exactly one resource group. The resources inside one group can live in different regions, but the group itself has a location (that location stores metadata about the group)."},{type:"example",title:"A resource group is a labeled box, not a building",body:"You might put a West Europe web app, a North Europe database, and a global Traffic Manager profile in the same resource group called rg-shop-prod. They are organized together. They are not forced into the same datacenter."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam point",points:["One resource → one resource group (never two at once)","Deleting the group deletes the resources in it","Great place to apply tags, locks, and RBAC for a whole app","Not a billing boundary — the subscription is"]},{type:"heading",text:"3. Subscriptions"},{type:"p",text:"A subscription is the billing boundary. Microsoft sends the invoice for a subscription. It is also an access and quota boundary: each subscription has limits (how many vCPUs, how many storage accounts) and is tied to a Microsoft Entra tenant for identity. Companies often use several subscriptions to split bills (prod vs sandbox), to isolate environments, or to stay under quota limits. Creating a resource always happens in a subscription."},{type:"trap",text:"A resource group does not get its own invoice. The subscription does. Resource groups organize. Subscriptions bill."},{type:"heading",text:"4. Management groups"},{type:"p",text:"Management groups sit above subscriptions. They exist so you can apply Azure Policy and role-based access control (RBAC) to many subscriptions at once, instead of repeating yourself. Every Entra tenant has a root management group. You can nest management groups (think “Contoso / Europe / Production”) and hang subscriptions underneath. Management groups do not host VMs. They are an organization and governance layer."},{type:"example",title:"One policy, many bills",body:"Finance wants every subscription in the company to deny public storage accounts. Put those subscriptions in a management group and assign the policy there. It inherits downward. You still get a separate invoice per subscription."},{type:"think",text:"Management group = folder of subscriptions. Use it for policy and RBAC at scale, not for deploying a VM."},{type:"heading",text:"5. What each level is for"},{type:"p",text:"Billing lives at the subscription. Day-to-day organization and “delete this app in one click” lives at the resource group. The actual workload is the resource. Governance that must cover lots of subscriptions lives at the management group. RBAC and Azure Policy can be assigned at management group, subscription, resource group, or (for RBAC) even a single resource — and they inherit down the tree. That inheritance is why the hierarchy exists."},{type:"table",caption:"Pick the level that matches the job",headers:["Level","Main job","Typical exam hint"],rows:[["Management group","Policy & RBAC across subscriptions","“Apply to all subscriptions”"],["Subscription","Billing, quotas, access boundary","“Who pays?” / “invoice”"],["Resource group","Organize resources, shared lifecycle","“Delete together” / “logical container”"],["Resource","The VM, disk, web app, VNet…","“The thing you created”"]]},{type:"diagram",title:"Inheritance flows downward",ascii:`  Management group  — Policy: "no public IPs"
          │ inherits
          ▼
     Subscriptions  — still billed separately
          │ inherits
          ▼
    Resource groups
          │ inherits
          ▼
       Resources    — the policy still applies here`},{type:"interactive",diagram:"resource-hierarchy"},{type:"trap",text:"You cannot put a subscription inside a resource group. The tree only goes management groups → subscriptions → resource groups → resources."},{type:"steps",items:[{title:"Management group",text:'"I need one policy or one set of admins across many subscriptions."'},{title:"Subscription",text:'"This is the bill, the quota, and the hard isolation boundary."'},{title:"Resource group",text:'"These resources belong together and should live and die together."'},{title:"Resource",text:'"This is the actual VM, storage account, or virtual network."'}]},{type:"heading",text:"Getting an Azure account"},{type:"p",text:"To create anything you need an Azure account, and that account gives you at least one subscription. Microsoft Learn names three ways to start: an Azure free account (free access to popular services for 12 months, an always-free tier of some services, and a credit to spend in the first 30 days), an Azure free student account (free services and a credit for 12 months, no credit card needed for verified students), and the Microsoft Learn sandbox (a temporary subscription created for you while you do a Learn exercise, cleaned up automatically)."},{type:"list",items:["Azure account → owns one or more subscriptions","Subscription → holds resource groups and is the billing boundary","Management groups → up to six levels deep under the root, to organize subscriptions"]},{type:"think",text:"Account → subscription(s) → resource groups → resources. Management groups sit above subscriptions to organize them."},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Azure free account",what:"12 months of popular free services plus a 30-day credit",remember:"Learning and trying things out"},{term:"Resource",what:"A single Azure thing you deploy",remember:"The item"},{term:"Resource group",what:"Logical container for resources",remember:"Organize + lifecycle, not billing"},{term:"Subscription",what:"Billing, quota, and access boundary",remember:"The invoice"},{term:"Management group",what:"Groups subscriptions for policy/RBAC",remember:"Governance above the bill"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Resources are the things you create, resource groups organize them, subscriptions bill and cap them, and management groups apply policy and RBAC across many subscriptions."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A company has 40 Azure subscriptions. Security wants every subscription to require MFA for admins and to block creation of public storage accounts, without setting that 40 times.",answer:"Put the subscriptions under a management group and assign Azure Policy (and RBAC) there.",why:"Management groups exist to govern many subscriptions at once. A resource group cannot contain subscriptions, and a single subscription policy would not cover the other 39."}],quiz:[{id:"rh-1",prompt:"What is a resource group?",options:["The invoice Microsoft sends each month","A logical container that holds related Azure resources","A physically separate datacenter inside a region","A folder that can contain other Azure subscriptions"],answer:1,explanation:"A resource group is a logical container. It is not a datacenter, not a bill, and it cannot hold subscriptions."},{id:"rh-2",prompt:"Which level of the Azure hierarchy is the billing boundary?",options:["Resource","Resource group","Subscription","Availability zone"],answer:2,explanation:"Microsoft bills per subscription. Resource groups organize resources; they do not get their own invoice.",trap:"People pick resource group because they “group” things. Grouping is not billing."},{id:"rh-3",prompt:"What can a management group contain?",options:["Virtual machines and storage accounts directly","Only resource groups","Subscriptions and other management groups","Availability zones"],answer:2,explanation:"Management groups sit above subscriptions. You nest management groups and attach subscriptions. You do not deploy VMs into a management group."},{id:"rh-4",prompt:"You delete a resource group in the portal. What happens to the resources inside it?",options:["They move automatically to another group","They stay running but become untagged","They are deleted along with the group","Only the virtual machines survive"],answer:2,explanation:"A resource group is a lifecycle boundary. Deleting it deletes the resources it contains."},{id:"rh-5",prompt:"A resource in Azure can belong to how many resource groups at the same time?",options:["Zero","Exactly one","Two, if they are in different regions","As many as you tag"],answer:1,explanation:"Each resource lives in exactly one resource group. You can move it later, but it is never in two groups at once."},{id:"rh-6",prompt:"You need to apply the same Azure Policy to every subscription in the company. Where is the most efficient place to assign it?",options:["On each virtual machine","On a single resource group called Policy","On a management group that contains those subscriptions","On an availability zone"],answer:2,explanation:"Policy assigned at a management group inherits to all subscriptions (and their resource groups and resources) underneath.",trap:"A resource group cannot contain subscriptions, so it cannot be the company-wide assignment point."},{id:"rh-7",prompt:"A company wants separate bills for its Marketing and Engineering departments. What is the simplest structural choice?",options:["Create a separate subscription for each department","Put both departments in one resource group","Create two availability zones","Apply a ReadOnly lock to each team"],answer:0,explanation:"Subscriptions are billing boundaries, so separate subscriptions give separate invoices. Tags can also split costs, but a subscription is the structural boundary."},{id:"rh-8",prompt:"Which Azure account option is designed for verified students and does not require a credit card?",options:["Azure free student account","Pay-as-you-go account","Enterprise Agreement","Microsoft Learn sandbox"],answer:0,explanation:"The Azure free student account gives free services and a credit for 12 months to verified students without a credit card. The Learn sandbox is temporary and only for Learn exercises."},{id:"rh-9",prompt:"How deep can a management group hierarchy go below the root management group?",options:["Two levels","Six levels","Unlimited levels","One level"],answer:1,explanation:"Management groups support up to six levels of depth, not counting the root level or the subscription level."},{id:"rh-10",prompt:"Which statement about resource groups is TRUE?",options:["Resource groups can be nested inside other resource groups","Resources in one resource group can be in different regions","Deleting a resource group keeps its resources","A resource group is the billing boundary"],answer:1,explanation:"A resource group is a logical container; its resources can live in different regions. Resource groups cannot nest, deleting one deletes its contents, and subscriptions are the billing boundary."}]},{slug:"compute",order:8,title:"Azure compute: VMs, containers, and functions",emoji:"💻",domain:"architecture",minutes:18,summary:"How Azure runs your code — virtual machines, containers, and Functions — plus VM options, the parts of a VM, and where web apps should live.",examFocus:"Match the compute type to the amount of control you want. Full OS control → VM. Package the app, skip the OS → container. Run a small piece of code when an event happens → Functions. Host a website without babysitting servers → App Service.",youWillLearn:["When to choose a VM, a container, or Azure Functions","What VM Scale Sets, availability sets, and Azure Virtual Desktop are for","Which extra resources a virtual machine needs","How App Service, containers, and VMs compare as app hosts","The AI, machine learning, and IoT/Edge service categories at a glance"],officialModule:{...o.compute,units:["Describe Azure virtual machines","Describe Azure virtual desktop","Describe Azure containers","Describe Azure functions","Describe AI, machine learning, and IoT/Edge services in Azure","Describe application hosting options"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"⚙️",text:"Compute is “where the code runs.” Azure gives you a spectrum: rent a whole computer, pack the app in a container, or hand Microsoft a function and let events wake it up. AZ-900 wants you to pick the right height on that spectrum."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Three compute types"},{type:"p",text:"A virtual machine is Infrastructure as a Service. You pick a size (CPU and memory), an OS image (Windows or Linux), and you patch and configure that OS. A container packages your app plus its libraries, without a full guest operating system for every copy — lighter, faster to start, easier to ship the same way everywhere. Azure Functions is serverless compute: you write a function, bind it to an event (HTTP call, queue message, timer), and Azure runs it. You do not manage servers, and you typically pay when the code actually runs."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Virtual machines",subtitle:"IaaS — you manage the OS",points:["Full control: custom software, specific OS settings","You patch, back up, and size the machine","Heaviest operational work, most flexibility"]},{title:"Containers",subtitle:"App + dependencies, not a full OS each time",points:["Consistent package from laptop to Azure","ACI for simple jobs; AKS when you need an orchestrator","You manage the app image, not a whole Windows/Linux VM OS"]},{title:"Functions",subtitle:"Serverless — event-driven snippets",points:["No servers to patch","Scales automatically with events","Best for short tasks, not a giant always-on monolith"]}]},{type:"think",text:"VM = rent a computer. Container = ship the app in a box. Function = run this code when X happens."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Virtual machine options"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Virtual Machines are the individual IaaS computers. Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets (VMSS) let you run a group of identical VMs that grow and shrink automatically — the exam picture for autoscale at the VM layer. An availability set places VMs in the same region across fault domains (different racks / power) and update domains (staggered host patching) so one rack or one host update does not take every VM down. Availability sets are the older in-datacenter high-availability pattern; availability zones are separate buildings. Azure Virtual Desktop (AVD) is Windows desktops and apps streamed from Azure — a desktop-as-a-service, not just “a VM you RDP into.”"},{type:"diagram",title:"Availability set vs scale set vs zone (exam memory)",ascii:`  Availability set     Scale set              Availability zones
  (same datacenter)    (identical VMs)        (separate buildings)
  FD1  FD2  FD3        VM VM VM VM …          Zone1  Zone2  Zone3
   VM   VM   VM        ▲ autoscale ▲            VM     VM     VM
  Survive a rack       Survive load spikes     Survive a datacenter
  or a host update     by adding more VMs      going dark`},{type:"trap",text:"An availability set is not an availability zone. Sets spread VMs inside a datacenter. Zones spread them across datacenters in the region."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam point",points:["Need many identical VMs that autoscale → VM Scale Sets","Need VMs to survive rack failure / host updates in one region → availability set","Need Windows desktops for remote users → Azure Virtual Desktop","Need one custom server you fully control → a regular Azure VM"]},{type:"heading",text:"3. Resources a VM needs"},{type:"p",text:"A VM is never just a CPU slider. At fundamentals level, remember the bundle: a VM size (vCPUs and memory), an OS disk (and optional data disks), a network interface (NIC), and a virtual network plus subnet for that NIC to plug into. You often add a public IP if it must be reached from the internet, and a network security group to filter traffic. The VM also needs an identity (admin username/password or SSH key) and sits in a resource group and region like everything else."},{type:"example",title:"Creating a VM is like moving into an apartment",body:"The size is how many rooms (CPU/RAM). The OS disk is the furnished kitchen that boots the OS. Extra data disks are storage closets. The NIC is the ethernet port. The VNet/subnet is the street and building. A public IP is the listed street address. Without the street (VNet), the apartment cannot exist."},{type:"list",items:["Compute: VM size — vCPU and memory","Storage: OS disk, optional data disks","Network: NIC, virtual network, subnet; optional public IP and NSG","Identity & place: admin credentials, resource group, region (and maybe a zone or availability set)"]},{type:"heading",text:"4. Application hosting options"},{type:"p",text:"When the question is “where should this web app run?”, Azure still offers three big answers. Azure App Service hosts web apps and APIs as Platform as a Service — you bring the code (or container), Microsoft handles the web server, patching, and scale. Containers (App Service, Azure Container Apps, Azure Container Instances, or AKS) fit when the app is already packaged as an image. Virtual machines fit when you need a custom runtime, unsupported OS settings, or software that expects a full machine. Same website, three levels of control."},{type:"diagram",title:"Who manages what for a web app",ascii:`  You manage →
  App Service (web apps) :  code / config
  Containers             :  image + code
  Virtual machines       :  OS + runtime + image + code
                            ▲ most control, most homework`},{type:"think",text:"Web app, do not want servers → App Service. Already in a container image → containers. Need a full custom OS → VM."},{type:"heading",text:"5. AI, machine learning, and IoT/Edge categories"},{type:"p",text:"The compute module now ends with a quick tour of three service families. You only need the category and the classic use case. Azure AI services (formerly Cognitive Services) are prebuilt AI models you call through an API — vision, speech, language, translation — no data science degree needed. Azure AI Foundry is the place to build and run generative AI apps with large models. Azure Machine Learning is the platform for data scientists to train, deploy, and manage their own models. Azure IoT Hub connects, monitors, and manages millions of IoT devices, and Azure IoT Edge runs cloud workloads (including AI) on the devices themselves, close to the data."},{type:"table",caption:"If the scenario says… think…",headers:["Need","Category / service"],rows:[["Call an API to read text in photos or transcribe speech","Azure AI services (prebuilt models)"],["Build a chat app on a large language model","Azure AI Foundry / Azure OpenAI"],["Data scientists train and deploy their own models","Azure Machine Learning"],["Connect and manage thousands of sensors","Azure IoT Hub"],["Run analysis on the factory device, even offline","Azure IoT Edge"]]},{type:"trap",text:"Prebuilt API (AI services) vs build-your-own model (Machine Learning) is the distinction to hold onto. IoT Hub is the cloud side; IoT Edge is the device side."},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Azure AI services",what:"Prebuilt AI models behind APIs",remember:"Vision, speech, language — no training"},{term:"Azure Machine Learning",what:"Platform to train and deploy your own models",remember:"For data scientists"},{term:"IoT Hub / IoT Edge",what:"Manage devices in the cloud / run workloads on devices",remember:"Cloud side vs device side"},{term:"Azure VM",what:"IaaS virtual computer",remember:"You manage the OS"},{term:"VM Scale Set",what:"Identical VMs that autoscale",remember:"More copies when busy"},{term:"Availability set",what:"Fault + update domains in a datacenter",remember:"Not the same as a zone"},{term:"Azure Virtual Desktop",what:"Cloud Windows desktops and apps",remember:"Remote desktop as a service"},{term:"Containers",what:"Packaged app without a full guest OS each",remember:"ACI simple, AKS orchestrated"},{term:"Azure Functions",what:"Serverless event-driven code",remember:"Pay when it runs"},{term:"App Service",what:"PaaS web apps and APIs",remember:"Host the website, skip the VM"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"VMs give you a full computer that needs CPU, memory, disks, and a NIC on a VNet; scale sets add identical autoscaling copies; availability sets spread VMs inside a datacenter; Virtual Desktop streams Windows workspaces; containers package apps; Functions run event-driven code; and App Service is the usual PaaS home for a web app."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A team has a .NET website, does not want to patch Windows Server, and needs the site to scale when traffic spikes. They are not bringing a custom kernel module.",answer:"Azure App Service (a web app).",why:"App Service is PaaS hosting for web apps. A VM would work but adds OS homework. Functions are a poor fit for a full website. Scale Sets are the VM-level autoscale answer, not the “we don’t want servers” answer."}],quiz:[{id:"cmp-1",prompt:"You want to run a small piece of code when a message arrives on a queue, without managing any servers. What should you use?",options:["An availability set","Azure Functions","Azure Virtual Desktop","A region pair"],answer:1,explanation:"Functions are serverless and event-driven. You bind code to an event and Azure runs it. No servers to patch."},{id:"cmp-2",prompt:"What is the main difference between a virtual machine and a container?",options:["Containers cannot run in Azure; VMs can","A VM virtualizes a full machine and OS; a container packages the app and its libraries without a full guest OS per instance","Containers always cost more than VMs","A container is the same thing as Azure Functions"],answer:1,explanation:"VMs include a guest operating system you manage. Containers share a host and ship the app plus dependencies in an image."},{id:"cmp-3",prompt:"A company needs many identical VMs that increase automatically during a sale. Which option is designed for that?",options:["Azure Virtual Desktop","A single availability set with one VM","Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets","Azure DNS"],answer:2,explanation:"VM Scale Sets run a pool of identical VMs and scale the count in and out. That is the autoscale story at the VM layer."},{id:"cmp-4",prompt:"What does an availability set protect you from?",options:["An entire Azure region going offline","Rack-level hardware failure and host updates inside a datacenter, by spreading VMs across fault and update domains","A forgotten password on the VM","The need to choose a VM size"],answer:1,explanation:"Availability sets use fault domains and update domains in a datacenter. Region-wide disasters are a region-pair / DR topic. Separate buildings are availability zones.",trap:"Do not pick “entire region.” That is what region pairs and geo-redundancy are for."},{id:"cmp-5",prompt:"Besides CPU and memory, which resources does a typical Azure VM also require?",options:["Only a management group","An OS disk, a network interface, and a virtual network/subnet to attach to","ExpressRoute and Azure Arc","An Azure Functions plan"],answer:1,explanation:"A VM needs disks and a NIC on a VNet/subnet. CPU and memory are the size — they are not the whole resource list."},{id:"cmp-6",prompt:"You want to host a website in Azure without managing the underlying web servers. Which hosting option matches that?",options:["A virtual machine you RDP into every week to patch IIS","Azure App Service (web apps)","Azure Virtual Desktop","An availability set"],answer:1,explanation:"App Service is PaaS for web apps: you bring the code, Microsoft runs the platform. AVD is for desktops, not public websites."},{id:"cmp-7",prompt:"A team wants to add image tagging and speech-to-text to an app by calling prebuilt models, without training anything. Which service family?",options:["Azure AI services","Azure Machine Learning","Azure IoT Hub","Azure Virtual Desktop"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure AI services expose prebuilt models through APIs. Azure Machine Learning is for building and training your own models."},{id:"cmp-8",prompt:"A factory needs to connect, monitor, and send commands to thousands of sensors from the cloud. Which service?",options:["Azure IoT Hub","Azure Functions","Azure DNS","Azure Machine Learning"],answer:0,explanation:"IoT Hub is the cloud gateway for registering, monitoring, and messaging IoT devices at scale."},{id:"cmp-9",prompt:"You need to run a single container quickly without managing any VMs or an orchestrator. Which service is the simplest fit?",options:["Azure Container Instances","Azure Kubernetes Service","Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets","Azure Virtual Desktop"],answer:0,explanation:"ACI is the fastest, simplest way to run a container in Azure — PaaS, no VMs to manage. AKS is for orchestrating many containers.",trap:"AKS is powerful, but “simplest single container” is ACI."},{id:"cmp-10",prompt:"Which service orchestrates many containers across a cluster, handling scaling and rolling updates?",options:["Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)","Azure Container Instances","Azure App Service plan","An availability set"],answer:0,explanation:"AKS is managed Kubernetes — the orchestrator for running many containers at scale."}]},{slug:"networking",order:9,title:"Azure networking fundamentals",emoji:"🌐",domain:"architecture",minutes:17,summary:"VNets, subnets, peering, Azure DNS, VPN Gateway versus ExpressRoute, and public versus private endpoints.",examFocus:"VPN Gateway = encrypted tunnel over the public internet. ExpressRoute = private connection that does not go over the public internet. Private endpoint = a private IP for a PaaS service inside your VNet.",youWillLearn:["What a virtual network and a subnet are for","How VNet peering connects two VNets","When to use Azure DNS","The difference between VPN Gateway, ExpressRoute, and public vs private endpoints","Basic network access controls: NSGs, Azure Firewall, and DDoS Protection"],officialModule:{...o.networking,units:["Describe Azure virtual networking","Describe Azure virtual private networks","Describe Azure ExpressRoute","Describe Azure DNS"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🔌",text:"If compute is where code runs, networking is how that code talks to users, to on-premises, and to other Azure services. AZ-900 keeps this at the map level: private networks, name resolution, and two ways to plug the office into Azure."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Virtual networks and subnets"},{type:"p",text:"An Azure virtual network (VNet) is your private network in Azure. You pick a private IP address space (for example 10.0.0.0/16) and a region. Resources such as VMs get private IPs from that VNet. A subnet slices the VNet into smaller ranges so you can separate workloads — web tier in one subnet, data tier in another — and attach different network security groups. Subnets live inside one VNet. You cannot stretch a single VNet across two regions; for two regions you create two VNets and connect them."},{type:"example",title:"A VNet is the private office LAN in the cloud",body:"The building is the region. The LAN is the VNet. Floors are subnets. Desks are VMs with private IPs. Visitors from the internet only get in if you add a public IP, a load balancer, or another published entry point."},{type:"diagram",title:"One VNet, two subnets",ascii:`  VNet  10.0.0.0/16     (East US)
  ┌────────────────────────────────┐
  │  subnet-web  10.0.1.0/24       │
  │     [VM] [VM]                  │
  │  subnet-data 10.0.2.0/24       │
  │     [VM] [DB]                  │
  └────────────────────────────────┘
  Private IPs. Not the public internet.`},{type:"think",text:"VNet = your private network in Azure. Subnet = a slice of that network."},{type:"heading",text:"2. VNet peering"},{type:"p",text:"VNet peering connects two Azure virtual networks so resources can talk using private IPs. Traffic stays on the Microsoft backbone — it is not hairpinned out to the public internet. You can peer VNets in the same region or in different regions (global peering). The VNets stay separate management objects; you are not merging them into one giant VNet. Address spaces must not overlap."},{type:"trap",text:"Peering is not a VPN Gateway and it is not ExpressRoute. Peering links Azure VNets to each other. VPN/ExpressRoute link Azure to somewhere else (usually on-premises)."},{type:"heading",text:"3. Azure DNS"},{type:"p",text:"DNS turns names into IP addresses. Azure DNS is a hosting service for DNS domains that runs on Azure’s name servers. You can host a public zone (contoso.com on the internet) or a private zone that resolves names only inside your VNets (app.internal.contoso). On the exam, Azure DNS is the answer when the need is “host and resolve DNS names in Azure,” not when the need is “connect two networks.”"},{type:"think",text:"Azure DNS = name tags. VNet = the private streets. They solve different problems."},{type:"heading",text:"4. VPN Gateway vs ExpressRoute"},{type:"p",text:"Both connect on-premises networks to Azure. They do it very differently. Azure VPN Gateway creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet (site-to-site for offices, point-to-site for individual laptops). It is usually faster to set up and cheaper. ExpressRoute is a private connection through a connectivity provider. Your traffic does not travel over the public internet. You get more reliability, more consistent bandwidth, and often lower latency — at higher cost and with more setup. Encrypted-over-internet vs private-circuit is the distinction Microsoft wants."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Azure VPN Gateway",subtitle:"Encrypted over the internet",points:["IPsec/IKE tunnels across the public internet","Site-to-site or point-to-site","Good starting hybrid option; bandwidth and latency depend on the internet"]},{title:"ExpressRoute",subtitle:"Private connection",points:["Dedicated circuit via a provider; not the public internet","More consistent performance and higher bandwidth options","More cost and lead time; used when internet VPN is not enough"]}]},{type:"diagram",title:"Two ways the office reaches Azure",ascii:`  On-premises                          Azure VNet
      │
      │  VPN Gateway:  ==== encrypted ====  (public internet)
      │
      │  ExpressRoute: ---- private fiber -- (not the public internet)
      │
      └───────────────────────────────────►  your subnets`},{type:"interactive",diagram:"hybrid-connectivity"},{type:"trap",text:"ExpressRoute is not “a faster VPN.” It is a different path: private connectivity. VPN Gateway still uses the internet; it just encrypts what it sends."},{type:"heading",text:"5. Public vs private endpoints"},{type:"p",text:"A public endpoint is an address reachable from the public internet (a public IP on a VM, or the public HTTPS URL of a storage account). A private endpoint gives a PaaS service a private IP on your VNet using Azure Private Link. Traffic to that storage account or database then stays on the private network instead of going out to a public hostname. Use public when the service must be reached from the internet. Use private when you want the service to look like it lives on your LAN."},{type:"example",title:"Same storage account, two front doors",body:"Public endpoint: https://mystuff.blob.core.windows.net from a café Wi-Fi. Private endpoint: 10.0.3.8 on subnet-privatelink, only reachable from your VNet (or from on-premises over VPN/ExpressRoute)."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam point",points:["VNet / subnet — private address space in Azure","Peering — VNet to VNet on the Microsoft backbone","Azure DNS — host and resolve names","VPN Gateway — encrypted hybrid over the internet","ExpressRoute — private hybrid connection","Private endpoint — private IP for a PaaS service in your VNet"]},{type:"heading",text:"6. Basic network access controls"},{type:"p",text:"Once resources are on a VNet you need to decide what traffic is allowed. A network security group (NSG) is a list of allow/deny rules (source, destination, port, protocol) attached to a subnet or a NIC — the basic filter that says “allow 443 from the internet, deny everything else.” Azure Firewall is a managed, stateful firewall service for the whole network: central rules, application (FQDN) filtering, threat intelligence, logging. Azure DDoS Protection defends public endpoints against distributed denial-of-service floods; a basic level is always on for the platform, and the paid tier adds tuning, alerts, and cost protection for your resources."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"NSG",subtitle:"Simple allow/deny rules",points:["Attached to a subnet or NIC","Port, protocol, IP rules","Free, basic filtering"]},{title:"Azure Firewall",subtitle:"Managed central firewall",points:["Stateful, whole-network","FQDN and threat-intel filtering","Paid, centrally logged"]},{title:"DDoS Protection",subtitle:"Stop traffic floods",points:["Protects public IPs","Absorbs volumetric attacks","Not a rule-based filter"]}]},{type:"trap",text:"An NSG does not stop a DDoS flood, and DDoS Protection does not decide which ports are open. Rules → NSG or Firewall. Floods → DDoS Protection."},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Network security group",what:"Allow/deny rules on a subnet or NIC",remember:"Basic port filter"},{term:"Azure Firewall",what:"Managed stateful firewall for the network",remember:"Central rules + FQDN filtering"},{term:"DDoS Protection",what:"Defends public endpoints from floods",remember:"Volume attacks, not port rules"},{term:"Virtual network",what:"Your private network in Azure",remember:"The LAN"},{term:"Subnet",what:"A slice of a VNet",remember:"A floor in the building"},{term:"VNet peering",what:"Private connection between two VNets",remember:"Azure to Azure, not a VPN"},{term:"Azure DNS",what:"Host DNS zones in Azure",remember:"Names → IP addresses"},{term:"VPN Gateway",what:"Encrypted tunnel over the internet",remember:"Hybrid, uses public internet"},{term:"ExpressRoute",what:"Private circuit to Azure",remember:"Hybrid, not the public internet"},{term:"Private endpoint",what:"Private IP for a PaaS service",remember:"Keep PaaS off the public internet"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"A VNet with subnets is your private Azure network, peering joins VNets on Microsoft’s backbone, Azure DNS resolves names, VPN Gateway encrypts hybrid traffic over the internet, ExpressRoute uses a private connection instead, and private endpoints put PaaS services on a private IP in your VNet."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A bank must connect its datacenter to Azure with a dedicated circuit that does not travel over the public internet, and it wants the Azure SQL database to be reachable only via a private IP on the VNet.",answer:"ExpressRoute plus a private endpoint for Azure SQL.",why:"ExpressRoute is the private on-premises path. A private endpoint gives the database a VNet IP. VPN Gateway would still ride the public internet, and a public endpoint would keep the database on the internet."}],quiz:[{id:"net-1",prompt:"What is an Azure virtual network (VNet)?",options:["A DNS hosting service","Your private network in Azure, with an address space you define","A physical fiber circuit from your office","A tool that copies blobs between storage accounts"],answer:1,explanation:"A VNet is the private IP network you create in a region. Subnets divide it. It is not DNS and it is not ExpressRoute."},{id:"net-2",prompt:"What does VNet peering do?",options:["Encrypts traffic from a laptop over the public internet","Connects two Azure virtual networks so they can communicate over the Microsoft backbone using private IPs","Assigns a public IP to every VM","Hosts the contoso.com DNS zone"],answer:1,explanation:"Peering links VNets. Traffic stays on Microsoft’s network. VPN Gateway is the internet-encrypted hybrid option."},{id:"net-3",prompt:"Which service is designed to host DNS domains and resolve names in Azure?",options:["ExpressRoute","Azure DNS","Availability sets","Azure Arc"],answer:1,explanation:"Azure DNS hosts public and private DNS zones. ExpressRoute is private connectivity, not name hosting."},{id:"net-4",prompt:"A company wants an encrypted connection from its office to Azure that travels across the public internet. What should it use?",options:["ExpressRoute","Azure VPN Gateway","VNet peering only","A sovereign region"],answer:1,explanation:"VPN Gateway builds an encrypted tunnel over the public internet. ExpressRoute is the private, not-the-internet path.",trap:"If the question says “does not use the public internet,” pick ExpressRoute. If it says “encrypted over the internet,” pick VPN Gateway."},{id:"net-5",prompt:"How is ExpressRoute different from a VPN Gateway?",options:["ExpressRoute is cheaper and always uses Wi-Fi","ExpressRoute provides a private connection through a provider and does not go over the public internet","ExpressRoute can only connect two Azure VNets in the same region","ExpressRoute is another name for Azure DNS"],answer:1,explanation:"ExpressRoute is private connectivity. VPN Gateway encrypts over the public internet. Peering is VNet-to-VNet."},{id:"net-6",prompt:"What is a private endpoint?",options:["A public IP address on a virtual machine","A private IP address on your VNet that maps to an Azure PaaS service (Private Link)","A DNS record in a public zone","A second Azure region used as a pair"],answer:1,explanation:"A private endpoint brings a service such as Storage or Azure SQL onto a private IP in your subnet so clients do not have to use the public endpoint."},{id:"net-7",prompt:"You need to allow inbound HTTPS to a web subnet and block all other inbound ports. What is the simplest control?",options:["A network security group (NSG) on the subnet","Azure DDoS Protection","ExpressRoute","Azure DNS"],answer:0,explanation:"NSGs hold allow/deny rules by port, protocol, and IP. DDoS Protection handles floods, not port rules."},{id:"net-8",prompt:"A public website is being hit with a massive flood of traffic designed to knock it offline. Which service is designed for this?",options:["Azure DDoS Protection","A network security group","VNet peering","A private endpoint"],answer:0,explanation:"Distributed denial-of-service attacks are absorbed and mitigated by Azure DDoS Protection."},{id:"net-9",prompt:"Which VPN Gateway connection type connects a single remote laptop to an Azure VNet?",options:["Point-to-site","Site-to-site","ExpressRoute Direct","VNet peering"],answer:0,explanation:"Point-to-site connects individual devices. Site-to-site connects a whole on-premises network."},{id:"net-10",prompt:"You want centrally managed, stateful network filtering across many VNets, including rules by fully qualified domain name. Which service?",options:["Azure Firewall","A network security group","Azure DNS","Azure Bastion"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure Firewall is a managed, stateful firewall service with central policy and FQDN filtering. NSGs are simpler, per-subnet/NIC rules."}]},{slug:"storage",order:10,title:"Azure Storage services, tiers, and moving data",emoji:"📦",domain:"architecture",minutes:19,summary:"Blob, File, Queue, Table, and Disk; Hot / Cool / Cold / Archive; LRS through RA-GZRS; storage accounts; AzCopy, Storage Explorer, File Sync; Azure Migrate and Data Box.",examFocus:"Match the data shape to the service (objects → Blob, SMB shares → Files, messages → Queue, NoSQL keys → Table, VM disks → Disk). Then match how often you read it (tier) and how many buildings must keep a copy (redundancy).",youWillLearn:["How Blob, File, Queue, Table, and Disk storage differ","What Hot, Cool, Cold, and Archive tiers are for","How LRS, ZRS, GRS, GZRS and the RA- variants keep copies","Which tool moves files or migrates whole workloads"],officialModule:{...o.storage,units:["Describe Azure storage accounts","Describe Azure storage redundancy","Describe Azure storage services","Identify Azure data migration options","Identify Azure file movement options"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"💾",text:"“Azure Storage” is a family, not one box. The exam will describe a kind of data and expect you to name the service, the access tier, the redundancy, or the copy tool. Learn the family first; the rest is matching."},{type:"heading",text:"1. The storage services"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Blob Storage holds unstructured objects — photos, videos, backups, logs, data-lake files. Azure Files gives you managed file shares you can mount with SMB or NFS, like a cloud file server. Azure Queue Storage is a simple message queue between applications. Azure Table Storage is a NoSQL key-attribute store (cheap wide tables, not a relational database). Azure Disk Storage provides the managed disks that VMs boot from and attach as data disks. Blob/File/Queue/Table usually live in a storage account. Disks are their own resource, attached to VMs."},{type:"table",caption:"Pick the service that matches the data",headers:["Service","What you store","Exam hint"],rows:[["Blob","Unstructured objects","Images, backups, data lake"],["Files","File shares (SMB/NFS)","Lift-and-shift a file server"],["Queue","Messages","App A tells app B later"],["Table","NoSQL key-attribute rows","Cheap structured data, not SQL"],["Disk","VM disks","OS disk / data disk"]]},{type:"trap",text:"Blob is objects, not a Windows drive letter. Files is the share you map as a network drive. Disk is what the VM believes is a local hard drive."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Storage accounts and types"},{type:"p",text:"A storage account is the unique namespace and settings box for blobs, files, queues, and tables (for example mystorageaccount.blob.core.windows.net). Standard general-purpose v2 is the account type Microsoft wants you to default to — it supports the current services, access tiers, and redundancy options. Premium accounts trade cost for performance: premium block blobs (low-latency objects), premium file shares, and premium page blobs (often for disks). Older account kinds (general-purpose v1, legacy Blob Storage accounts) may appear as “not the recommended default.”"},{type:"think",text:"General-purpose v2 = the standard storage account for AZ-900. Premium = faster and more expensive for a specific workload."},{type:"heading",text:"3. Access tiers (Hot, Cool, Cold, Archive)"},{type:"p",text:"Blob access tiers trade storage price against access price. Hot: data you read and write often — highest storage price, lowest access price. Cool: infrequent access, typically kept at least 30 days. Cold: rarely accessed but still online with millisecond retrieval, typically kept at least 90 days — cheaper than Cool to store, more expensive to read. Archive: offline, cheapest to store, hours to rehydrate, typically 180 days minimum. Cold is an online tier. Archive is the offline one. That distinction is a favorite trap."},{type:"diagram",title:"Tiers from “touch it daily” to “maybe never”",ascii:`  Hot  →  Cool  →  Cold  →  Archive
  often    sometimes  rarely   almost never
  online   online     online   offline (hours to retrieve)
  $ storage high →→→→→→→→→→→  $ storage low
  $ access  low  →→→→→→→→→→→  $ access high`},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam point",points:["Hot / Cool / Cold / Archive are the current blob access tier names","Cold is online (fast retrieve); Archive is offline (hours)","Early retrieval can cost extra if you have not met the minimum days","Tiers are about how often you access blobs — not about LRS vs GRS"]},{type:"heading",text:"4. Redundancy options"},{type:"p",text:"Redundancy is how many copies Azure keeps, and where. LRS (locally redundant storage): three copies in one datacenter. ZRS (zone-redundant): copies across availability zones in the region. GRS (geo-redundant): LRS in the primary region plus async copies to the paired region. GZRS (geo-zone-redundant): ZRS in the primary plus copies to the secondary region. Add RA- in front (RA-GRS, RA-GZRS) and you get read access to the secondary region even before a failover — handy for read-heavy disaster-recovery designs. Conceptually: L = local building, Z = zones, G = another region, RA = you can read the other region."},{type:"diagram",title:"Where the copies live",ascii:`  LRS    :  three copies, one datacenter
  ZRS    :  copies across zones in one region
  GRS    :  primary region  ~~~~copy~~~~>  paired region
  GZRS   :  zones in primary  ~~~~copy~~~~>  paired region
  RA-GRS / RA-GZRS : same as GRS / GZRS, plus you can read the secondary`},{type:"interactive",diagram:"storage-redundancy"},{type:"trap",text:"GRS does not by itself let you read the secondary while the primary is healthy. That extra is the RA- (read-access) variants."},{type:"heading",text:"5. Moving files"},{type:"p",text:"AzCopy is a command-line tool built for high-throughput copies to and from Azure Storage (and between accounts). Azure Storage Explorer is the graphical app for browsing blobs, files, queues, and tables — same data, friendlier clicks. Azure File Sync sits on a Windows Server and syncs its files with an Azure Files share, with optional cloud tiering so old files live in Azure while the server keeps placeholders. File Sync is the “keep my on-prem file server, but back it with Azure Files” answer, not a one-shot copy."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"AzCopy",subtitle:"CLI",points:["Scripts and big copies","Fast, automatable","No GUI required"]},{title:"Storage Explorer",subtitle:"GUI",points:["Click to upload, download, browse","Great for people, not scheduled jobs"]},{title:"Azure File Sync",subtitle:"Ongoing sync",points:["Windows Server ↔ Azure Files","Cloud tiering","Not a bulk blob migration tool"]}]},{type:"heading",text:"6. Migration: Azure Migrate and Azure Data Box"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Migrate is the hub for discovering, assessing, and migrating workloads (servers, databases, web apps, and more) into Azure over the network. Azure Data Box is a family of physical devices Microsoft ships to you: you copy a large dataset onto the device, ship it back, and Microsoft uploads it into Azure. Use Data Box when the dataset is so large that copying over the WAN would take forever or cost too much. Migrate = plan and move systems. Data Box = truck the bits when the network is the bottleneck."},{type:"example",title:"When the WAN is the problem",body:"Eighty terabytes of video archives would take weeks on the office internet. Order a Data Box, copy the files in the datacenter, ship it to Microsoft. Meanwhile Azure Migrate can still assess the VMs you will lift-and-shift next month."},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Blob / Files / Queue / Table / Disk",what:"Object, share, message, NoSQL, VM disk",remember:"Match the data shape"},{term:"Hot / Cool / Cold / Archive",what:"Blob access tiers",remember:"Cold is online; Archive waits hours"},{term:"LRS / ZRS / GRS / GZRS",what:"How copies are spread",remember:"Building / zones / paired region"},{term:"RA-GRS / RA-GZRS",what:"Geo redundancy plus read on secondary",remember:"RA = read access"},{term:"General-purpose v2",what:"Recommended storage account type",remember:"Default for most scenarios"},{term:"AzCopy / Explorer / File Sync",what:"CLI, GUI, Windows file-server sync",remember:"How you move or sync files"},{term:"Azure Migrate / Data Box",what:"Assess-and-migrate vs ship a device",remember:"Network path vs offline path"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Pick Blob, Files, Queue, Table, or Disk for the data shape, a general-purpose v2 account unless you need premium, a Hot–Cool–Cold–Archive tier for how often you touch blobs, LRS/ZRS/GRS/GZRS (and RA- for reads on the secondary) for how many places keep copies, AzCopy or Storage Explorer or File Sync to move files, and Azure Migrate or Data Box when you are migrating whole workloads or too much data for the network."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A studio has 60 TB of finished videos they almost never watch, must keep for seven years, and their office internet cannot upload that much this quarter. They still want cheap storage once the data is in Azure.",answer:"Azure Data Box to ingest, then Blob Storage on the Archive (or Cold, if they need millisecond retrieve) tier.",why:"Data Box solves the offline bulk transfer. Archive is the cheapest blob tier when hours of retrieval are acceptable. AzCopy over that internet would not finish in time; Azure Migrate is for workloads, not a pile of video files."}],quiz:[{id:"st-1",prompt:"Which Azure Storage service is designed for unstructured objects such as images, backups, and video files?",options:["Azure Disk Storage","Azure Queue Storage","Azure Blob Storage","Azure Virtual WAN"],answer:2,explanation:"Blob Storage is object storage. Disks attach to VMs. Queues store messages. Files is for SMB/NFS shares."},{id:"st-2",prompt:"A lift-and-shift app expects an SMB file share like the old Windows file server. Which service matches that?",options:["Azure Files","Azure Queue Storage","Azure Functions","ExpressRoute"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure Files provides managed file shares over SMB (and NFS). Blob is objects; it is not a native SMB share."},{id:"st-3",prompt:"You need the cheapest blob storage for data you almost never read and can wait hours to retrieve. Which tier?",options:["Hot","Cool","Cold","Archive"],answer:3,explanation:"Archive is the offline tier: lowest storage cost, hours to rehydrate. Cold is still online with millisecond access.",trap:"Cold vs Archive is the 2020s trap. Cold is online. Archive is offline."},{id:"st-4",prompt:"Which redundancy option keeps copies in a single datacenter?",options:["GZRS","LRS (locally redundant storage)","RA-GRS","ZRS (zone-redundant storage)"],answer:1,explanation:"LRS replicates three times inside one datacenter. ZRS uses zones. GRS/GZRS add a paired region. RA- adds read access to that secondary."},{id:"st-5",prompt:"You want a graphical tool to upload and browse blobs, and a command-line tool for scripted high-speed copies. Which pair?",options:["Azure Arc and Azure Advisor","Azure Storage Explorer and AzCopy","Azure DNS and ExpressRoute","Availability sets and scale sets"],answer:1,explanation:"Storage Explorer is the GUI. AzCopy is the CLI. File Sync is ongoing Windows Server synchronization, not the generic GUI/CLI pair."},{id:"st-6",prompt:"A factory must get 100 TB of files into Azure, but the WAN is too slow. They also need a service to assess on-premises servers for a later VM migration. Which pair of services?",options:["Azure DNS and Azure Firewall","Azure Data Box and Azure Migrate","Azure Functions and Azure Virtual Desktop","Resource locks and Azure Policy"],answer:1,explanation:"Data Box is the offline ship-a-device path for huge datasets. Azure Migrate is the assessment and migration hub for workloads."},{id:"st-7",prompt:"Which storage account type is the recommended default for most scenarios?",options:["Standard general-purpose v2","General-purpose v1","Legacy Blob Storage account","Premium page blobs"],answer:0,explanation:"Standard general-purpose v2 supports blobs, files, queues, tables, all access tiers, and all redundancy options. Premium types are for specific high-performance workloads."},{id:"st-8",prompt:"A branch office wants to keep its Windows file server, but have files backed by Azure and old files tiered to the cloud. Which service?",options:["Azure File Sync","AzCopy","Azure Data Box","Azure Migrate"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure File Sync syncs a Windows Server with an Azure Files share and can tier cold files to the cloud. AzCopy is a one-off copy tool."},{id:"st-9",prompt:"Which redundancy option protects against a full regional outage AND an availability-zone failure in the primary region?",options:["GZRS","LRS","ZRS","GRS"],answer:0,explanation:"GZRS = ZRS in the primary (zone protection) plus copies to a secondary region (regional protection). GRS uses LRS in the primary, so it does not protect against a zone failure there."},{id:"st-10",prompt:"Two applications need to pass messages asynchronously so one can process work later. Which storage service fits?",options:["Azure Queue Storage","Azure Table Storage","Azure Disk Storage","Azure Files"],answer:0,explanation:"Queue Storage holds messages between application components. Table is NoSQL data, Disk is VM disks, Files is SMB/NFS shares."}]},{slug:"identity",order:11,title:"Azure identity: Microsoft Entra ID",emoji:"🪪",domain:"architecture",minutes:18,summary:"Microsoft Entra ID, Entra Domain Services, SSO, MFA, passwordless, external identities, and Conditional Access — who you are before anyone asks what you can do.",examFocus:"Microsoft renamed Azure AD to Microsoft Entra ID. If a question still says Azure AD, it means Entra ID. Do not mix Entra ID (cloud directory) with Entra Domain Services (managed legacy domain).",youWillLearn:["What Microsoft Entra ID is — and why it used to be called Azure AD","When Microsoft Entra Domain Services shows up instead of Entra ID","SSO, MFA, and passwordless in plain language","External identities and Conditional Access as exam-style if/then rules"],officialModule:{...o.identity,units:["Describe Azure directory services","Describe Azure authentication methods","Describe Azure external identities","Describe Azure conditional access"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🪪",text:"Before Azure lets anyone create a virtual machine or open a storage account, it wants to know who they are. That is identity. This lesson is the guest list, the badges, and the extra checks at the door — not the job titles that decide what you can do once you are inside."},{type:"p",text:"Keep one pair of words straight from the first minute. Authentication is proving who you are (the badge). Authorization is what you are allowed to do (the job title). This lesson is mostly authentication. Azure role-based access control — the authorization side — gets its own lesson next."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD)"},{type:"p",text:"Microsoft Entra ID is Azure's cloud identity and access management directory. It stores users, groups, and applications. When someone signs in to the Azure portal, Microsoft 365, or thousands of other apps, Entra ID is usually the service checking the badge. It used to be named Azure Active Directory, or Azure AD. Same product, new name. The exam may still say Azure AD. Answer as if it said Microsoft Entra ID."},{type:"think",text:"Microsoft Entra ID = the cloud guest list. Users, groups, and apps live here. It is not a virtual machine and it is not a firewall."},{type:"trap",text:'Never treat "Azure AD" as a different product. Azure AD was renamed to Microsoft Entra ID. If a question says Azure AD, it means Entra ID.'},{type:"heading",text:"2. Microsoft Entra Domain Services"},{type:"p",text:"Some older apps still speak the language of on-premises Active Directory Domain Services: domain join, LDAP, Kerberos, NTLM, Group Policy. Microsoft Entra Domain Services is a Microsoft-managed domain for those apps. You do not stand up your own domain controllers. Microsoft runs them. Lift-and-shift a legacy app onto Azure VMs, and it can still behave as if it were on a classic domain."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Microsoft Entra ID",subtitle:"Cloud directory",points:["Users, groups, and modern apps","Sign-in with protocols such as OAuth, OpenID Connect, and SAML","SSO, MFA, Conditional Access","The default identity service for Azure and Microsoft 365"]},{title:"Microsoft Entra Domain Services",subtitle:"Managed legacy domain",points:["LDAP, Kerberos, NTLM, Group Policy","Domain-join Azure VMs that need classic AD","Microsoft manages the domain controllers","For apps that cannot speak modern cloud identity yet"]}]},{type:"trap",text:"Entra Domain Services is not “Entra ID with extra features.” It is a managed traditional domain for legacy apps. If the scenario says LDAP, Kerberos, or domain-join without managing DCs, think Domain Services — not Entra ID alone."},{type:"heading",text:"3. Authentication: SSO, MFA, and passwordless"},{type:"p",text:"Single sign-on (SSO) means you sign in once and then open many apps without typing the password again. One badge, many doors. Multifactor authentication (MFA) means Azure wants a second proof — something you know (password), something you have (phone or hardware key), or something you are (fingerprint or face). Passwordless goes further: you sign in with Windows Hello, a FIDO2 security key, or the Microsoft Authenticator app, without a password at all."},{type:"diagram",title:"Three ways to prove it is you",ascii:`SSO                 MFA                    Passwordless
────                ───                    ────────────
One sign-in         Password               Windows Hello
opens many apps     + phone / key / face   FIDO2 key
                                           Authenticator app`},{type:"think",text:"SSO = convenience across apps. MFA = extra proof. Passwordless = proof without a password. They can work together: SSO that still demands MFA when the sign-in looks risky."},{type:"heading",text:"4. External identities"},{type:"p",text:"Not everyone who signs in works at your company. External identities let people outside your organization use their own credentials with your apps. AZ-900 usually paints two pictures. Business-to-business (B2B) is a guest user: a partner or contractor you invite into your directory. Business-to-consumer (B2C / customer identity) is people signing up for your public app — shoppers, patients, citizens — not employees."},{type:"example",title:"Two different “outsiders”",body:"A vendor needs access to your Azure DevOps project: invite them as a guest (B2B). A customer creates an account on your shopping website with their Google or email login: that is customer / B2C identity. Same idea — people who are not employees — different relationship."},{type:"heading",text:"5. Microsoft Entra Conditional Access"},{type:"p",text:"Conditional Access is an if/then policy engine on top of Entra ID. IF a sign-in looks a certain way, THEN require extra proof or block it. Signals include the user, the app, the location, the device, and the risk of the sign-in. It is not a network firewall. It is identity making a decision at sign-in time."},{type:"example",title:"A classic if/then",body:"IF a finance user signs in from an unknown country, THEN require MFA. IF the device is not marked compliant, THEN block access to the payroll app. You are not rewriting the network. You are adding a smarter bouncer."},{type:"diagram",title:"Conditional Access in one glance",ascii:`Sign-in attempt
      │
      ▼
 Signals: user \xb7 location \xb7 device \xb7 app \xb7 risk
      │
      ▼
 Conditional Access policy
      │
      ├── Allow
      ├── Require MFA / compliant device
      └── Block`},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["Microsoft Entra ID is the cloud identity directory (formerly Azure AD)","Microsoft Entra Domain Services = managed AD DS for legacy apps","SSO = one sign-in, many apps","MFA = more than one proof of identity","Passwordless = Windows Hello, FIDO2, Authenticator — no password","External identities = guests and customers, not only employees","Conditional Access = if this sign-in, then this extra requirement"]},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Microsoft Entra ID",what:"Cloud identity directory (formerly Azure AD)",remember:"The guest list for Azure and Microsoft 365"},{term:"Entra Domain Services",what:"Managed domain with LDAP / Kerberos",remember:"Legacy apps, Microsoft runs the DCs"},{term:"SSO",what:"Single sign-on",remember:"One login, many apps"},{term:"MFA",what:"Multifactor authentication",remember:"Password plus something else"},{term:"Passwordless",what:"Sign-in without a password",remember:"Hello, FIDO2, Authenticator"},{term:"External identities",what:"People outside your org",remember:"Guests (B2B) and customers (B2C)"},{term:"Conditional Access",what:"If/then rules at sign-in",remember:"Not a firewall — identity policy"}]},{type:"steps",items:[{title:"Cloud users and modern apps",text:"Microsoft Entra ID (the exam may still say Azure AD)."},{title:"LDAP, Kerberos, domain-join, no DC to manage",text:"Microsoft Entra Domain Services."},{title:"Sign in once, open Outlook, Teams, and the portal",text:"SSO."},{title:"Password plus a phone prompt",text:"MFA."},{title:"Invite a partner into your tenant",text:"External identity / B2B guest."},{title:"Require MFA when the location looks strange",text:"Conditional Access."}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD) is the cloud directory that authenticates people; Domain Services covers legacy domain apps; SSO, MFA, and passwordless are how you prove it is you; external identities bring guests and customers in; Conditional Access adds if/then rules on top of the sign-in."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A company moves an old line-of-business app to Azure virtual machines. The app must domain-join and use Kerberos. The team does not want to manage domain controllers.",answer:"Microsoft Entra Domain Services",why:"That is the managed traditional domain. Entra ID handles modern cloud sign-in; it does not replace LDAP/Kerberos for that legacy app."}],quiz:[{id:"id-1",prompt:"A question on the exam mentions Azure Active Directory (Azure AD). What is that product called today?",options:["Microsoft Defender for Cloud","Microsoft Entra ID","Microsoft Entra Domain Services","Azure Policy"],answer:1,explanation:"Azure AD was renamed to Microsoft Entra ID. Same directory service, new name. Domain Services is a different product for legacy domain workloads.",trap:"Do not pick Domain Services just because it also has Entra in the name."},{id:"id-2",prompt:"An app on Azure VMs needs LDAP and Kerberos, and the company does not want to run its own domain controllers. What should they use?",options:["Microsoft Entra ID only","Resource locks","Microsoft Entra Domain Services","Azure Advisor"],answer:2,explanation:"Entra Domain Services is a Microsoft-managed domain that speaks LDAP, Kerberos, and NTLM. Entra ID is the cloud directory for modern authentication."},{id:"id-3",prompt:"Users sign in once and can open Microsoft 365, the Azure portal, and a SaaS app without typing the password again. What is that?",options:["Conditional Access","Single sign-on (SSO)","Azure RBAC","Defense-in-depth"],answer:1,explanation:"SSO is one authentication unlocking many apps. Conditional Access decides extra requirements. RBAC is authorization after you are signed in."},{id:"id-4",prompt:"A company wants staff to sign in with Windows Hello or a FIDO2 security key instead of a password. What method is that?",options:["Passwordless authentication","Azure Policy","Resource Health","A ReadOnly lock"],answer:0,explanation:"Passwordless uses Windows Hello, FIDO2 keys, or the Microsoft Authenticator app so there is no password to steal or type."},{id:"id-5",prompt:"You invite a contractor from another company to access a few of your Azure resources using their own work account. What is this an example of?",options:["Microsoft Entra Domain Services","An external identity (B2B guest)","Azure Service Health","A resource lock"],answer:1,explanation:"External identities cover people outside your organization. Inviting a partner or contractor as a guest is the B2B picture. B2C is customers signing up for your app."},{id:"id-6",prompt:"IF a user signs in from an unknown location, THEN require MFA. Which feature is that?",options:["Azure Monitor alerts","Microsoft Entra Conditional Access","Azure tags","Network security groups"],answer:1,explanation:"Conditional Access is identity if/then policy at sign-in. NSGs filter network traffic. Monitor alerts watch metrics and logs. Tags are metadata.",trap:"Conditional Access is not a firewall. It evaluates the sign-in, not packets."},{id:"id-7",prompt:"Which is the best description of multifactor authentication (MFA)?",options:["Requiring two or more forms of verification, such as a password plus a phone prompt","Using the same password for several apps","Signing in once to reach many apps","Giving a user the Owner role"],answer:0,explanation:"MFA combines something you know, something you have, and/or something you are. Signing in once to reach many apps is SSO."},{id:"id-8",prompt:"Which of these is a passwordless sign-in option in Microsoft Entra ID?",options:["Microsoft Authenticator app sign-in","A longer password","SMS-only password reset","A shared admin account"],answer:0,explanation:"Passwordless options include Windows Hello for Business, the Microsoft Authenticator app, and FIDO2 security keys."},{id:"id-9",prompt:"Authentication vs authorization — which pairing is correct?",options:["Authentication proves who you are; authorization decides what you can do","Authentication decides what you can do; authorization proves who you are","They are the same thing","Authorization only applies to SaaS"],answer:0,explanation:"AuthN = identity (Entra ID sign-in, MFA). AuthZ = permissions (Azure RBAC)."},{id:"id-10",prompt:"A company wants customers (not employees) to sign up to its public app with social or email accounts. Which Entra capability fits?",options:["External identities for customers (Entra External ID)","Microsoft Entra Domain Services","Azure RBAC","A resource lock"],answer:0,explanation:"External identities cover both B2B guests (partners) and customer-facing sign-up (B2C / External ID for customers)."}]},{slug:"security",order:12,title:"Azure access and security",emoji:"🛡️",domain:"architecture",minutes:16,summary:"RBAC, Zero Trust, defense-in-depth, encryption with Azure Key Vault, and Microsoft Defender for Cloud — plus a light contrast with Policy and locks so you do not mix the three tools.",examFocus:"RBAC answers who can do what. Policy answers what is allowed to exist. Locks answer what cannot be deleted. Zero Trust is a mindset. Defender for Cloud is the security score and protection service.",youWillLearn:["How Azure RBAC uses roles and scopes","The Zero Trust idea in exam language","Defense-in-depth as stacked layers, not one magic product","What Microsoft Defender for Cloud is for — and how RBAC differs from Policy and locks","Encryption at rest and in transit, and what Azure Key Vault stores"],officialModule:{...o.identity,units:["Describe Azure role-based access control","Describe Zero Trust model","Describe defense-in-depth","Describe encryption and key management in Azure","Describe Microsoft Defender for Cloud"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"🛡️",text:"Identity proved who you are. Security is what happens next: what you are allowed to do, how Azure thinks about trust, how layers pile up, and which service watches your security posture. The exam loves mixing these names. We will keep them in separate drawers."},{type:"p",text:"Picture an office building. The badge at the door is authentication (last lesson). Your job title — receptionist, electrician, CEO — is authorization. Azure's job-title system is role-based access control. Around that sit two bigger ideas: never assume the person in the hallway is safe (Zero Trust), and never rely on a single lock (defense-in-depth)."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Azure role-based access control (RBAC)"},{type:"p",text:"Azure RBAC is authorization: who can do what, on which scope. You assign a role to a user, group, or service principal, at a management group, subscription, resource group, or single resource. Built-in roles you will see on the exam include Owner (full control, including access), Contributor (create and manage, but not grant access), and Reader (look but do not change). The habit Microsoft wants is least privilege: the smallest role on the smallest scope that still gets the job done."},{type:"diagram",title:"RBAC scope inherits downward",ascii:`Management group
        │  (role here applies to everything below)
        ▼
   Subscription
        │
        ▼
   Resource group
        │
        ▼
     Resource`},{type:"example",title:"Least privilege in one sentence",body:"A developer needs to restart web apps in the “dev” resource group. Give them Contributor (or a tighter web-app role) on that resource group — not Owner on the whole subscription."},{type:"think",text:"RBAC = job title + floor of the building. Owner / Contributor / Reader are the titles. Management group → subscription → resource group → resource are the floors."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Zero Trust"},{type:"p",text:"Old model: if you are on the corporate network, we trust you. Zero Trust says the opposite. Never trust, always verify. Being “inside” is not enough. Every request is checked. Three phrases show up again and again: verify explicitly, use least privilege, assume breach. MFA, Conditional Access, and tight RBAC are how Azure puts that idea to work — but Zero Trust itself is the model, not a single checkbox named Zero Trust."},{type:"list",items:["Verify explicitly — authenticate and authorize using all the signals you have (identity, device, location, risk).","Least privilege — just enough access, just in time, just the right scope.","Assume breach — design as if an attacker is already inside, so one stolen password is not the whole company."]},{type:"think",text:"Zero Trust is a castle where even the king shows a badge at every door. It is a strategy, not a product SKU."},{type:"heading",text:"3. Defense-in-depth"},{type:"p",text:"Defense-in-depth means stacked layers. If one layer fails, the next one still has a chance. Azure describes layers from the physical datacenter up through identity, perimeter, network, compute, application, and data. A firewall alone is not defense-in-depth. MFA plus NSGs plus encryption plus RBAC plus physical security — that is the model."},{type:"diagram",title:"Layers, not one wall",ascii:`  ┌─────────────────────────────┐
  │ Physical (datacenter)       │
  │  Identity & access          │
  │   Perimeter                 │
  │    Network                  │
  │     Compute                 │
  │      Application            │
  │       Data                  │
  └─────────────────────────────┘
         If one layer fails,
         the others still stand.`},{type:"heading",text:"4. Encryption and key management"},{type:"p",text:"Encryption scrambles data so only someone with the key can read it. Azure talks about two states. Encryption at rest protects stored data — Azure Storage, managed disks, and Azure SQL Database encrypt data at rest by default with Microsoft-managed keys. Encryption in transit protects data moving across a network — TLS/HTTPS for web traffic, plus encrypted tunnels like VPN Gateway. If you must control the keys yourself (for compliance), you can use customer-managed keys stored in Azure Key Vault."},{type:"p",text:"Azure Key Vault is the safe for secrets: application secrets (connection strings, passwords, API keys), cryptographic keys, and TLS/SSL certificates. Apps fetch what they need at runtime instead of hard-coding a password in a config file, access is controlled with Entra ID and RBAC, and every use is logged. Managed HSM is the option when you need keys held in dedicated hardware security modules."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"At rest",subtitle:"Data sitting on disk",points:["Storage, disks, databases","On by default with Microsoft-managed keys","Customer-managed keys optional"]},{title:"In transit",subtitle:"Data moving on a network",points:["TLS / HTTPS","VPN Gateway tunnels","Protects against eavesdropping"]},{title:"Azure Key Vault",subtitle:"Where keys and secrets live",points:["Secrets, keys, certificates","Access via Entra ID + RBAC","Audited every time it is used"]}]},{type:"trap",text:"Key Vault stores secrets; it does not encrypt your whole storage account by itself, and it is not an identity provider. And “encrypted at rest by default” does not make your data public-proof — access is still your job."},{type:"heading",text:"5. Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{type:"p",text:"Microsoft Defender for Cloud is Azure's cloud security posture and workload protection service. It looks at how you configured things, gives you a Secure Score, and recommends fixes (turn on MFA, close a public storage account, patch a VM). It can also protect workloads — servers, databases, containers — in Azure and, with extra setup, in hybrid and other clouds. Think of it as a security advisor that also helps shield the workloads, not as RBAC and not as a firewall you draw on a VNet."},{type:"think",text:"Defender for Cloud = “how secure is my estate, and what should I fix?” Azure Advisor also gives security tips, but Defender for Cloud is the dedicated security posture and protection service."},{type:"heading",text:"6. RBAC vs Policy vs locks (light contrast)"},{type:"p",text:"Three tools sit near each other on the exam. They are not interchangeable. You will meet Policy and locks in more depth in the governance lesson. For now, only the contrast matters."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"RBAC",subtitle:"Who can do what",points:["Assign roles to people and apps","Owner, Contributor, Reader, and many more","Scoped to MG / subscription / RG / resource"]},{title:"Azure Policy",subtitle:"What is allowed",points:["Rules such as “only these regions”","Can audit or deny non-compliant resources","About configuration, not job titles"]},{title:"Resource locks",subtitle:"Cannot delete (or cannot change)",points:["CanNotDelete or ReadOnly","Even an Owner must remove the lock first","A padlock, not a permission set"]}]},{type:"trap",text:"RBAC does not stop someone with Contributor from deleting a VM they are allowed to manage. A lock does. Policy does not assign the Contributor role. RBAC does. If the question is “who may create VMs,” that is RBAC. If it is “nobody may create VMs in East US,” that is Policy. If it is “do not let anyone delete this production vault,” that is a lock."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["RBAC = authorization: roles + scope, least privilege","Zero Trust = never trust, always verify (verify explicitly, least privilege, assume breach)","Defense-in-depth = many layers, so one failure is not game over","Microsoft Defender for Cloud = security posture (Secure Score) and workload protection","Do not confuse Defender for Cloud with Entra ID or with Azure Policy"]},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Azure Key Vault",what:"Safe for secrets, keys, and certificates",remember:"No passwords in config files"},{term:"Encryption at rest / in transit",what:"Stored data / data moving on a network",remember:"Disk vs wire (TLS)"},{term:"Azure RBAC",what:"Who can do what, where",remember:"Job title + scope"},{term:"Least privilege",what:"Smallest role, smallest scope",remember:"Not Owner on the subscription “just in case”"},{term:"Zero Trust",what:"Never trust, always verify",remember:"A model, not a product"},{term:"Defense-in-depth",what:"Stacked security layers",remember:"Moat + walls + guards + locked chest"},{term:"Defender for Cloud",what:"Posture + workload protection",remember:"Secure Score and recommendations"},{term:"Policy / locks",what:"Allowed config / cannot delete",remember:"Not the same as RBAC"}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"RBAC decides who can do what at which scope, Zero Trust refuses to trust a request just because it is “inside,” defense-in-depth stacks layers so one failure is not fatal, and Defender for Cloud scores and helps protect the estate — while Policy and locks handle allowed configuration and delete protection, not job titles."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A security lead wants a single Azure service that shows a Secure Score, flags a storage account that is open to the internet, and can add extra protection to VMs and databases.",answer:"Microsoft Defender for Cloud",why:"That is posture management plus workload protection. Advisor gives broader recommendations including cost. RBAC only assigns roles. Conditional Access only evaluates sign-ins."}],quiz:[{id:"sec-1",prompt:"What does Azure RBAC primarily control?",options:["Which regions a resource is allowed to use","Who can perform which actions on a scope","Whether a resource can be deleted","Whether a sign-in from another country requires MFA"],answer:1,explanation:"RBAC is authorization: roles on a scope. Policy covers allowed configuration (such as regions). Locks cover delete/change. Conditional Access covers sign-in if/then rules.",trap:"“Who can do what” is RBAC. “What is allowed to exist” is Policy."},{id:"sec-2",prompt:"You need a teammate to view everything in a resource group but not change it. Which built-in role fits?",options:["Owner","Contributor","Reader","User Access Administrator"],answer:2,explanation:"Reader is look-but-do-not-change. Contributor can change resources but not grant access. Owner can do both, including access."},{id:"sec-3",prompt:"Which statement best describes Zero Trust?",options:["Trust every request that comes from the corporate network","Never trust, always verify — even “inside” traffic is checked","Put every workload in a single network security group","Turn off MFA once a user is on a VPN"],answer:1,explanation:"Zero Trust assumes the network is hostile. Verify explicitly, least privilege, assume breach. Being on the VPN is not a free pass."},{id:"sec-4",prompt:"Defense-in-depth means:",options:["Buying one very strong firewall and nothing else","Using several independent security layers so one failure is not enough","Assigning every user the Owner role","Storing passwords in a spreadsheet as a backup layer"],answer:1,explanation:"Layers: physical, identity, perimeter, network, compute, application, data. One product is not the model."},{id:"sec-5",prompt:"Which service is designed to improve cloud security posture and protect workloads?",options:["Azure Pricing Calculator","Microsoft Defender for Cloud","Azure Cloud Shell","Azure Arc"],answer:1,explanation:"Defender for Cloud provides Secure Score, recommendations, and workload protection. The others are cost, command-line, and hybrid management tools."},{id:"sec-6",prompt:"You must stop anyone — including Owners — from accidentally deleting a production key vault until a second person removes a protection. What do you use?",options:["An Azure Policy initiative","The Reader role","A resource lock (CanNotDelete)","Microsoft Entra Domain Services"],answer:2,explanation:"Locks block delete (or all changes) even for Owners until the lock is removed. Policy governs configuration. Reader cannot protect against an Owner. Domain Services is identity for legacy apps."},{id:"sec-7",prompt:"Developers keep database connection strings in application config files. Where should these secrets be stored instead?",options:["Azure Key Vault","Azure Advisor","A resource tag","Azure DNS"],answer:0,explanation:"Key Vault securely stores secrets, keys, and certificates with access control and auditing. Tags are plain-text labels.",trap:"Never put a secret in a tag — tags are visible metadata."},{id:"sec-8",prompt:"HTTPS protects a user's data as it travels from the browser to an Azure web app. What is this an example of?",options:["Encryption in transit","Encryption at rest","Role-based access control","A resource lock"],answer:0,explanation:"TLS/HTTPS encrypts data moving on the network — in transit. At rest is about stored data."},{id:"sec-9",prompt:"Which of the following is one of the three principles of Zero Trust?",options:["Assume breach","Trust the internal network","Use one firewall","Share admin accounts"],answer:0,explanation:"Zero Trust principles: verify explicitly, use least-privilege access, assume breach."},{id:"sec-10",prompt:"What is Secure Score in Microsoft Defender for Cloud?",options:["A measurement of your security posture, with recommendations to raise it","The SLA of a virtual machine","The monthly cost estimate for security services","The number of users with MFA disabled by Microsoft"],answer:0,explanation:"Secure Score summarizes how well your resources follow security recommendations. Fixing recommendations raises the score."}]},{slug:"cost-management",order:13,title:"Azure cost management",emoji:"💰",domain:"governance",minutes:16,summary:"What makes an Azure bill go up, how the pricing calculator estimates before you deploy, what Cost Management does after you spend, and why tags are labels — not locks.",examFocus:"Pricing Calculator = estimate before you buy. Cost Management = actual spend after you run. Tags = name/value labels for grouping cost. Tags are not resource locks.",youWillLearn:["The factors that actually move an Azure bill","When to use the Pricing Calculator (before you deploy, not after)","What Cost Management can do with budgets and analysis","Why tags exist — and why they are not locks","Cost optimization: Reservations, savings plans, Spot, and Azure Hybrid Benefit"],officialModule:{...o.cost,units:["Describe factors that can affect costs in Azure","Explore the pricing calculator","Describe the Microsoft Cost Management tool","Describe the purpose of tags","Describe cost optimization options in Azure"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"💰",text:"Azure is a consumption service. You pay for what you use, where you use it, and how you committed to pay. This lesson is the grocery list, the estimate before you shop, the receipt after, and the sticky notes that say which department bought the milk."},{type:"p",text:"Three clocks matter. Before you deploy, you estimate. After resources are running, you watch real spend. Across both, you label resources so finance can see who owns which line on the bill. Mix those clocks up and the exam will catch you."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Factors that affect costs"},{type:"p",text:"The same VM size is not the same price everywhere. Region matters. Resource type and SKU matter (a tiny B-series VM is not an M-series monster). Usage meters matter: hours running, storage gigabytes, transactions, and especially bandwidth. Inbound data to Azure is often free or cheap; outbound data leaving Azure is where bills surprise people. How you buy matters too: pay-as-you-go, reservations or savings plans (commit for 1 or 3 years, lower rate), Spot VMs (spare capacity, can be evicted), and Azure Hybrid Benefit (reuse eligible Windows Server or SQL licenses)."},{type:"list",items:["Resource type and SKU — what you run, and how big it is","Region — prices differ by geography","Meters / usage — time, storage, operations","Bandwidth (egress) — data leaving Azure","Purchase model — pay-as-you-go, reservations, Spot, Hybrid Benefit","Redundancy and extras — geo-replication, support plans, marketplace images"]},{type:"diagram",title:"Bandwidth in one picture",ascii:`  On-prem / internet          Azure
         │                      │
         │   inbound (often free)
         │ ───────────────────► │
         │                      │
         │   outbound (usually $)
         │ ◄─────────────────── │
         │                      │
         Traffic between regions can also cost.`},{type:"trap",text:"“We turned the VM off in the portal” still leaves disks, public IPs, and some other resources billing. Stopped (deallocated) is the state that stops VM compute charges. Also: a reservation is a billing discount, not a different kind of virtual machine."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Pricing Calculator"},{type:"p",text:"The Azure Pricing Calculator is a public website. You add the services you are thinking about, pick region and size, and it estimates a monthly cost before anything exists in your subscription. It is a planning tool. It does not see your real bill."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Pricing Calculator",subtitle:"Before you deploy",points:["Estimate proposed Azure resources","No subscription required to play with it","Great for “what would this architecture cost?”"]},{title:"Azure Migrate business case",subtitle:"On-prem vs Azure",points:["Replaces the retired standalone TCO Calculator","Compares running servers on-prem vs in Azure","Migration business case — not your live bill"]},{title:"Cost Management",subtitle:"After you spend",points:["Real usage and invoices","Budgets, alerts, cost analysis","Lives in the Azure portal against your account"]}]},{type:"think",text:"Pricing Calculator = shopping-cart estimate. Azure Migrate business case = “should we leave the old datacenter?” (older material calls this the TCO Calculator). Cost Management = looking at the receipt."},{type:"heading",text:"3. Cost Management capabilities"},{type:"p",text:"Microsoft Cost Management (often shown with Billing) is where you analyze actual spend. You can break costs by subscription, resource group, service, and tag. You can set a budget — for example, $5,000 this month on the marketing subscription — and alerts when you hit 80% or 100%. You can export data to a storage account for finance tools. It can show recommendations, but it does not deploy architecture for you and it does not lock a resource from deletion."},{type:"exam",title:"What Cost Management is for",points:["Cost analysis of real Azure spend","Budgets and alerting when you approach a limit","Breakdowns by tag, resource, and service","Helping people take action — it does not replace RBAC or Policy"]},{type:"heading",text:"4. The purpose of tags"},{type:"p",text:"A tag is a name/value label you attach to a resource or resource group: Department = Finance, Project = Apollo, Environment = Prod. Azure can roll costs up by those labels so each team sees its slice of the bill. Tags are also handy for operations (“who owns this?”). They are metadata. They do not change how the resource runs."},{type:"example",title:"A tag that finance will actually use",body:"Every resource gets CostCenter = 1042 and Owner = ada@contoso.com. Cost Management can then show “how much did cost center 1042 spend in March?” without renaming resource groups."},{type:"trap",text:"Tags are not resource locks. A tag will not stop someone from deleting a VM. A lock will. Tags are also not Policy — though Policy can require that a tag exists. If the question is “group costs by department,” the answer is tags. If it is “prevent deletion,” the answer is a lock."},{type:"think",text:"Tags = sticky notes. Useful, cheap, easy to ignore if nobody applies them. Locks = padlocks. Different object, different job."},{type:"heading",text:"5. Cost optimization options"},{type:"p",text:"The official module now ends with ways to pay less for the same work. Azure Reservations: commit to a specific resource (for example a VM size in a region) for 1 or 3 years and get a large discount — best for steady, predictable workloads. Azure savings plan for compute: commit to a fixed hourly spend on compute for 1 or 3 years; the discount applies across VM sizes, regions, and some PaaS compute, so it is more flexible than a reservation. Spot pricing: use spare Azure capacity at a deep discount, but Azure can evict the VM when it needs the capacity back — only for interruptible jobs. Azure Hybrid Benefit: reuse Windows Server and SQL Server licenses you already own (with Software Assurance) to cut the price. And the everyday habits: right-size VMs, shut down or deallocate what is idle, and act on Azure Advisor's cost recommendations."},{type:"table",caption:"Pick the discount that fits the workload",headers:["Option","You commit to","Best for"],rows:[["Reservations","A specific resource for 1 or 3 years","Steady VMs that never change size or region"],["Savings plan for compute","A fixed hourly compute spend for 1 or 3 years","Steady spend, but sizes or regions change"],["Spot","Nothing — can be evicted","Batch, test, interruptible work"],["Azure Hybrid Benefit","Licenses you already own","Existing Windows Server / SQL Server licenses"]]},{type:"trap",text:"Reservation = locked to a resource type and region. Savings plan = locked to a spend amount, flexible on what you run. Spot is never the answer for production that must stay up."},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Savings plan for compute",what:"Commit to hourly compute spend for 1 or 3 years",remember:"Flexible across sizes and regions"},{term:"Region / SKU / meters",what:"Core cost factors",remember:"Where, how big, how much you used"},{term:"Bandwidth",what:"Data leaving Azure",remember:"Egress is the usual surprise"},{term:"Reservations / savings plans / Spot / Hybrid Benefit",what:"Ways to pay less",remember:"Commit, spare capacity, or reuse licenses"},{term:"Pricing Calculator",what:"Estimate before deploy",remember:"Shopping list, not the receipt"},{term:"Cost Management",what:"Actual spend, budgets, alerts",remember:"After resources are running"},{term:"Tags",what:"Name/value metadata",remember:"Cost allocation — not a lock"}]},{type:"steps",items:[{title:"“What would this architecture cost?”",text:"Pricing Calculator."},{title:"“Is Azure cheaper than our datacenter?”",text:"An Azure Migrate business case (formerly the TCO Calculator)."},{title:"“We are at 90% of this month's budget.”",text:"Cost Management budgets and alerts."},{title:"“Show spend by department.”",text:"Tags, then Cost Management analysis."},{title:"“Do not let anyone delete this.”",text:"A resource lock — not a tag."}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Azure cost depends on what you run, where you run it, how much you use (especially egress), and how you pay; the Pricing Calculator estimates before you deploy, Cost Management watches the real bill, and tags label resources for that bill without protecting them from deletion."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A finance partner wants each Azure resource labeled so next quarter's invoice can be split by department. They also ask whether those labels will stop engineers from deleting production databases.",answer:"Use tags for the split; they will not prevent deletion. Add resource locks if you need delete protection.",why:"Tags are metadata for grouping and reporting. Locks are the padlock. The exam loves offering tags as a fake answer to a deletion question."}],quiz:[{id:"cost-1",prompt:"Which factor commonly changes the price of the same VM size?",options:["The name of the resource group","The Azure region you deploy to","Whether the resource has a lock","Whether Azure Advisor is enabled"],answer:1,explanation:"Prices vary by region. Resource group names, locks, and Advisor do not set the meter rate."},{id:"cost-2",prompt:"Which traffic is most likely to appear as a surprise on an Azure bill?",options:["Inbound data to Azure from the internet","Outbound data leaving Azure (egress)","Traffic inside a single virtual network in one region","Reading a free documentation page"],answer:1,explanation:"Egress — data leaving Azure — is the classic bandwidth cost. Inbound is often free. Same-VNet traffic in one region is typically not billed like internet egress."},{id:"cost-3",prompt:"You want a monthly estimate for a proposed architecture before anything is deployed. Which tool?",options:["Azure Cost Management","Azure Pricing Calculator","Azure Service Health","Microsoft Purview"],answer:1,explanation:"Pricing Calculator estimates proposed resources. Cost Management analyzes actual spend after you have usage. Service Health is about incidents. Purview is data governance.",trap:"Calculator = before. Cost Management = after."},{id:"cost-4",prompt:"A team wants an alert when a subscription hits 80% of a $10,000 monthly spend limit. What do they use?",options:["A CanNotDelete lock","Microsoft Entra Conditional Access","A budget in Cost Management","Azure Cloud Shell"],answer:2,explanation:"Cost Management budgets watch real spend and can alert you. Locks prevent delete. Conditional Access is identity. Cloud Shell is a terminal."},{id:"cost-5",prompt:"What is the purpose of Azure tags?",options:["To prevent a resource from being deleted","To attach name/value metadata for organization and cost allocation","To encrypt a storage account","To assign the Owner role"],answer:1,explanation:"Tags are labels. They help you group and report. They are not locks, not encryption, and not RBAC."},{id:"cost-6",prompt:"A colleague says, “I tagged the VM Environment=Prod, so nobody can delete it.” What is wrong?",options:["Nothing — tags act as CanNotDelete locks","Tags are metadata only; a resource lock is what prevents deletion","Tags only work on subscriptions, not VMs","You must tag the region instead"],answer:1,explanation:"Tags do not protect resources. Apply a CanNotDelete or ReadOnly lock if you need that protection. Policy can require tags, but that still is not a lock."},{id:"cost-7",prompt:"A company commits to spend a fixed amount per hour on compute for 3 years, but wants the discount to apply even when it changes VM sizes and regions. What should it buy?",options:["An Azure savings plan for compute","An Azure Reservation for one VM size","Spot VMs","A Cost Management budget"],answer:0,explanation:"Savings plans commit to a spend amount and apply flexibly across compute. Reservations are tied to a specific resource type and region."},{id:"cost-8",prompt:"A company already owns Windows Server licenses with Software Assurance. Which option lowers the cost of running Windows VMs in Azure?",options:["Azure Hybrid Benefit","Azure Spot pricing","Geo-redundant storage","Azure Arc"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure Hybrid Benefit lets you bring eligible on-premises Windows Server and SQL Server licenses to Azure at a reduced rate."},{id:"cost-9",prompt:"A nightly batch job can be stopped and restarted at any time. Which option gives the deepest compute discount?",options:["Spot VMs","Pay-as-you-go VMs","Premium SSD disks","Dedicated hosts"],answer:0,explanation:"Spot VMs use spare capacity at a large discount and can be evicted — perfect for interruptible batch work."},{id:"cost-10",prompt:"Which Azure service gives personalized recommendations such as resizing or shutting down underused VMs to save money?",options:["Azure Advisor","Azure DNS","Microsoft Purview","Azure Service Health"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure Advisor's Cost category recommends right-sizing, shutting down idle resources, and buying reservations. Cost Management surfaces those recommendations too."}]},{slug:"governance",order:14,title:"Governance and compliance in Azure",emoji:"⚖️",domain:"governance",minutes:17,summary:"Microsoft Purview for data governance, Azure Policy for the rules of the estate, resource locks for the padlock, the Service Trust Portal for audit evidence — and the famous mix-up with RBAC, budgets, and retired Blueprints.",examFocus:"Purview catalogs and governs data. Policy enforces resource rules. Locks stop delete or change. RBAC is who. Budgets are money. Blueprints are legacy — not the current exam star.",youWillLearn:["What Microsoft Purview is for at AZ-900 depth","How Azure Policy enforces standards","CanNotDelete vs ReadOnly locks","How to separate Policy, RBAC, locks, budgets, and Blueprints on a question","What the Service Trust Portal gives auditors and compliance teams"],officialModule:{...o.governance,units:["Describe the purpose of Microsoft Purview","Describe the purpose of Azure Policy","Describe the purpose of resource locks","Describe the purpose of the Service Trust portal"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"⚖️",text:"Governance is how a company keeps Azure from turning into a junk drawer. You need to know what data you have, you need rules for how resources are built, and you need a padlock on the things that must not vanish. Three services. Three jobs. The exam will try to swap their name badges."},{type:"p",text:"A useful picture: Purview is the librarian who knows which books exist and whether they are confidential. Azure Policy is the building code (“no wooden wiring, only these regions”). Resource locks are the glass case over the rare book — even the librarian cannot yank it out without unlocking it first."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Microsoft Purview"},{type:"p",text:"Microsoft Purview is Microsoft's data governance family. At AZ-900 level, remember the purpose, not every blade in the portal. Purview helps you discover, catalog, classify, and understand data across Azure and beyond — a data map and catalog, lineage, and compliance visibility so you can answer “where is our customer data, and who may use it?” It is not the tool that assigns the Contributor role, and it is not the tool that blocks a VM SKU."},{type:"think",text:"Purview = data librarian + catalog + compliance visibility. If the scenario is about sensitive data, catalogs, or “what data do we even have?”, think Purview."},{type:"trap",text:"Purview is not Azure Policy. Policy governs how Azure resources are configured. Purview governs data: find it, classify it, understand it. If the question never mentions data, Purview is probably the wrong answer."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Azure Policy"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Policy enforces rules on resources. Allowed locations. Allowed VM SKUs. Require a tag. Deny public IP addresses on NICs. You assign a policy (or a group of them, called an initiative) at a management group, subscription, or resource group, and it inherits down. Effects you will hear about include Audit (flag it), Deny (block the create or update), and deploy-if-not-exists (put a setting in place). Policy does not log people in and it does not grant Owner."},{type:"example",title:"A policy you will recognize on the exam",body:"“Resources may only be created in West Europe and North Europe.” Someone tries East US. Policy can deny the deployment. RBAC is irrelevant here — even an Owner can be stopped by Policy."},{type:"diagram",title:"Policy inherits down the hierarchy",ascii:`Management group  ← assign “allowed locations”
        │
        ▼
   Subscriptions
        │
        ▼
   Resource groups
        │
        ▼
   Resources   (must follow the rule)`},{type:"think",text:"Policy = the rulebook for resources. RBAC = who is allowed to try. A person can have permission (RBAC) and still be denied by Policy."},{type:"heading",text:"3. Resource locks"},{type:"p",text:"A resource lock is a padlock you put on a subscription, resource group, or resource. There are two types. CanNotDelete means you can still read and modify, but you cannot delete. ReadOnly means you cannot delete and you cannot change — you can only read. Even an Owner has to remove the lock before they can break the protection. That is the point: slow down accidents."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"CanNotDelete",subtitle:"The usual production padlock",points:["Read: yes","Change settings: yes","Delete: no, until the lock is removed"]},{title:"ReadOnly",subtitle:"Hands off",points:["Read: yes","Change settings: no","Delete: no"]}]},{type:"trap",text:"A lock is not a backup. It does not copy your data. It does not replace RBAC — people still need roles to do their jobs. And a tag named DoNotDelete is just a sticky note. Only a lock is a lock."},{type:"heading",text:"4. The Service Trust Portal"},{type:"p",text:"The Microsoft Service Trust Portal (servicetrust.microsoft.com) is where Microsoft publishes the evidence of how its cloud services meet security, privacy, and compliance standards: independent audit reports (ISO 27001, SOC 1/2/3, PCI DSS, FedRAMP), whitepapers, and compliance guides. You sign in with a Microsoft cloud account to download most documents. It is a library of proof for auditors — not a tool that configures or checks your own resources."},{type:"think",text:"Auditor asks “show me Microsoft's SOC 2 report” → Service Trust Portal. Want to check YOUR resources against a standard → Azure Policy / Defender for Cloud regulatory compliance."},{type:"heading",text:"5. The mix-up table: keep these five apart"},{type:"p",text:"This is the highest-value page in the lesson. Microsoft will offer two of these as distractors on almost every governance question. Azure Blueprints used to package templates, Policy, and RBAC together. Blueprints are retired / legacy. Do not study them as a current feature. If they appear, they are usually a nostalgic wrong answer — not the star of today's exam."},{type:"table",caption:"If the question sounds like… pick…",headers:["Need","Tool","Not this"],rows:[["Who can start this VM?","RBAC","Policy"],["Only these regions / SKUs / tags","Azure Policy","RBAC"],["Nobody deletes this vault","Resource lock","Tags or budgets"],["Alert me at 80% of spend","Budget (Cost Management)","Policy"],["Catalog sensitive data","Microsoft Purview","Advisor"],["Download Microsoft's audit reports","Service Trust Portal","Azure Policy"],["Old “package of env templates”","Blueprints (legacy — skip)","Bicep / templates + Policy"]]},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["Purview = data governance, catalog, compliance visibility","Azure Policy = resource rules that audit or deny","Locks = CanNotDelete or ReadOnly, even against Owners","RBAC is who; Policy is what; locks are the padlock; budgets are money","Blueprints are not the current thing to memorize"]},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Service Trust Portal",what:"Microsoft's audit reports and compliance documents",remember:"Proof for auditors, not a config tool"},{term:"Microsoft Purview",what:"Data governance and catalog",remember:"Where is the data, and is it sensitive?"},{term:"Azure Policy",what:"Rules for resource configuration",remember:"Allowed locations, required tags, deny SKUs"},{term:"Initiative",what:"A set of policies grouped together",remember:"A policy pack"},{term:"CanNotDelete",what:"Lock that blocks delete only",remember:"You can still change the resource"},{term:"ReadOnly",what:"Lock that blocks delete and change",remember:"Look, do not touch"},{term:"Blueprints",what:"Retired / legacy packaging tool",remember:"Not the current exam star"}]},{type:"steps",items:[{title:"Data catalog / classification / lineage",text:"Microsoft Purview."},{title:"Force a standard on resources",text:"Azure Policy."},{title:"Stop accidental delete of production",text:"CanNotDelete lock."},{title:"Freeze a resource so it cannot change",text:"ReadOnly lock."},{title:"Grant a person Contributor",text:"RBAC — not Policy."},{title:"Warn at a spend threshold",text:"A budget, not a lock and not Policy."}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Purview governs data, Azure Policy governs how resources must look, resource locks padlock delete or change, RBAC still decides who may try, budgets watch money, and Blueprints are a legacy name you should not treat as today's answer."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"Leadership wants every new resource blocked unless it is in Canada Central or Canada East, and they also want the production Recovery Services vault protected so even Owners cannot delete it on a Friday night.",answer:"Azure Policy for the allowed locations; a CanNotDelete lock on the vault.",why:"Two different problems. Policy is the rule for the estate. The lock is the padlock on one precious resource. RBAC alone cannot express “no East US” or “Owners still cannot delete.”"}],quiz:[{id:"gov-1",prompt:"A company wants a catalog of data sources, classification of sensitive information, and visibility for compliance teams. Which service?",options:["Azure Policy","Microsoft Purview","A ReadOnly lock","Azure Cloud Shell"],answer:1,explanation:"Microsoft Purview is data governance: catalog, map, classification, compliance visibility. Policy is for resource configuration. Locks protect resources from change or delete."},{id:"gov-2",prompt:"You must ensure virtual machines can be created only with approved SKUs. What do you assign?",options:["The Reader role","Azure Policy","A Cost Management budget","Microsoft Entra Domain Services"],answer:1,explanation:"Allowed SKUs is a configuration rule — Azure Policy. Reader is RBAC. A budget watches money. Domain Services is legacy identity."},{id:"gov-3",prompt:"A lock is applied to a resource group as CanNotDelete. What can a Contributor still do?",options:["Delete the resource group","Read and modify resources, but not delete them","Nothing — all actions are blocked","Remove other people's RBAC assignments only"],answer:1,explanation:"CanNotDelete allows read and update; it blocks delete. ReadOnly would also block changes. The Contributor still needs their RBAC role; the lock is extra protection on top."},{id:"gov-4",prompt:"Which pairing is correct?",options:["RBAC = what resources are allowed; Policy = who can act","RBAC = who can act; Policy = what configuration is allowed","Locks = who can act; RBAC = cannot delete","Budgets = cannot delete; locks = spend alerts"],answer:1,explanation:"RBAC is authorization (who). Policy is the resource rulebook (what). Locks are padlocks. Budgets are money alerts.",trap:"This swap is the most common AZ-900 governance trap."},{id:"gov-5",prompt:"You need a resource that nobody can change or delete until a lock is removed. Which lock?",options:["CanNotDelete","ReadOnly","A Department tag","An Owner role"],answer:1,explanation:"ReadOnly blocks both change and delete. CanNotDelete still allows modifications. Tags and roles are not locks."},{id:"gov-6",prompt:"An older blog post says to use Azure Blueprints to wrap Policy and RBAC into one environment package. For today's AZ-900, what should you remember?",options:["Blueprints are the main governance tool you must configure","Blueprints are retired / legacy and not the current exam star — use Policy, RBAC, templates/Bicep, and locks","Blueprints replaced Microsoft Purview","Blueprints are a type of resource lock"],answer:1,explanation:"Blueprints are legacy. Current study focus is Policy, locks, Purview, RBAC, and modern deployment tools such as Bicep — not Blueprints as the hero product."},{id:"gov-7",prompt:"An auditor asks for Microsoft's latest SOC 2 and ISO 27001 reports for Azure. Where do you get them?",options:["Microsoft Service Trust Portal","Azure Advisor","Azure Pricing Calculator","Azure Resource Manager"],answer:0,explanation:"The Service Trust Portal publishes Microsoft's independent audit reports, compliance guides, and whitepapers."},{id:"gov-8",prompt:"What is an Azure Policy initiative?",options:["A group of policy definitions assigned together toward a goal","A type of resource lock","A role that grants Owner access","A budget for a subscription"],answer:0,explanation:"An initiative bundles related policy definitions (for example everything needed for a compliance standard) so you assign and track them as one."},{id:"gov-9",prompt:"A policy that requires a tag is assigned after resources already exist. What happens to the existing non-compliant resources?",options:["They are flagged as non-compliant, and can be fixed with remediation","They are deleted automatically","Nothing — policies only ever apply to subscriptions","They are moved to another region"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure Policy evaluates existing resources and reports compliance. Remediation tasks can fix many of them. Policy does not delete resources."},{id:"gov-10",prompt:"A lock is applied at the resource group level. What happens to resources in that group?",options:["They inherit the lock","Only the resource group is locked, not the resources","The lock is ignored for Owners","Locks cannot be applied to resource groups"],answer:0,explanation:"Locks inherit down: a lock on a subscription or resource group applies to every resource inside it, for every user including Owners."}]},{slug:"manage-and-deploy",order:15,title:"Managing and deploying Azure resources",emoji:"🧰",domain:"governance",minutes:18,summary:"Portal, Cloud Shell, CLI, PowerShell, Arc, ARM, ARM templates, and Bicep — and how they all fit together.",examFocus:"Microsoft loves mixing these names. If you can tell a management tool from a deployment language from Azure's control plane, you will pick up easy points.",youWillLearn:["The difference between Cloud Shell, CLI, and PowerShell","What ARM actually is (hint: not a command-line tool)","Why Bicep exists if ARM templates already exist","What Azure Arc is — and what it is not"],officialModule:{...o.manageDeploy,units:["Describe tools for interacting with Azure","Describe the purpose of Azure Arc","Describe Azure Resource Manager and Azure ARM templates"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"☁️",text:"This topic is very important for AZ-900, because Microsoft gives you several ways to manage and deploy Azure resources, and some of the names can be confusing. The easiest way is to separate them into management tools and deployment / infrastructure tools."},{type:"p",text:"First, understand the big picture. You have Azure resources like virtual machines, storage accounts, virtual networks, databases, and App Services. You need ways to create, configure, manage, and deploy them. Microsoft gives you several tools for doing that."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Azure Cloud Shell"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Cloud Shell is a command-line environment inside your browser. Instead of installing tools on your computer, you can open Cloud Shell directly from the Azure portal. Inside it, you can use Azure CLI or Azure PowerShell."},{type:"example",title:"A command you might type in Cloud Shell",body:"You are typing commands, but you are doing it inside Microsoft's cloud environment — not on a terminal you installed yourself.",code:"az group list"},{type:"think",text:"Cloud Shell = a terminal in your browser."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam point",points:["Accessible through a browser — no local install required","No need to configure Azure tools on your laptop","Supports Azure CLI","Supports Azure PowerShell"]},{type:"trap",text:"Cloud Shell is not a new language. It is the place you type. CLI and PowerShell are the languages you type in."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Azure CLI"},{type:"p",text:"CLI means Command-Line Interface. Azure CLI lets you manage Azure resources by typing commands. It is command-based, cross-platform, and available on Windows, Linux, and macOS."},{type:"example",title:"Create a resource group with Azure CLI",body:"This creates a resource group named MyResourceGroup in West Europe.",code:"az group create --name MyResourceGroup --location westeurope"},{type:"think",text:"Azure CLI = commands starting with az. az vm create. az group create. az storage account create."},{type:"exam",title:"Exam memory",points:["CLI → az commands"]},{type:"heading",text:"3. Azure PowerShell"},{type:"p",text:"Azure PowerShell is another way to manage Azure resources, using PowerShell commands. Both CLI and PowerShell can accomplish similar things. The difference is primarily the command-line environment and the syntax."},{type:"example",title:"The same resource group, in PowerShell",body:"Notice the difference. CLI looks like az group create. PowerShell looks like New-AzResourceGroup.",code:"New-AzResourceGroup -Name MyResourceGroup -Location WestEurope"},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Azure CLI",subtitle:"az …",points:["Commands start with az","Feels like a typical Linux/macOS CLI","Great if you already like bash-style commands"]},{title:"Azure PowerShell",subtitle:"Az cmdlets",points:["Commands look like New-AzResourceGroup","Verb-Noun style (New, Get, Set, Remove)","Great if you already live in PowerShell and automation"]}]},{type:"think",text:"Azure CLI → az. Azure PowerShell → Az PowerShell commands."},{type:"heading",text:"4. Azure Arc"},{type:"p",text:"This one is different. Azure Arc allows you to manage resources that are outside Azure using Azure management capabilities. Your company might have servers in Azure, servers in its own datacenter, and servers running in another cloud. Azure Arc can help you manage those resources through Azure."},{type:"diagram",title:"Azure Arc reaches outside Azure",ascii:`                 Azure
                   ☁️
                   |
              Azure Arc
             /    |     \\
            /     |      \\
       On-prem   AWS    Other environments`},{type:"trap",text:'Azure Arc does not mean "move everything into Azure." It extends Azure management to resources that stay where they are.'},{type:"think",text:"Arc = Azure reaches outside Azure. Manage hybrid and multicloud resources from Azure."},{type:"heading",text:"5. ARM — Azure Resource Manager"},{type:"p",text:"ARM is the management layer of Azure. When you create or manage Azure resources, ARM handles creating, updating, and deleting them. It also handles access control, resource organization, tags, locks, and policies."},{type:"diagram",title:"Everything goes through ARM",ascii:`You
 ↓
Azure Portal / CLI / PowerShell
 ↓
Azure Resource Manager (ARM)
 ↓
Azure Resources`},{type:"p",text:"So when you use the Azure portal to create a VM, ARM is involved behind the scenes. When you use Azure CLI (az vm create), ARM is also involved."},{type:"think",text:"ARM is not just another command-line tool. It is the management layer that receives requests and manages Azure resources."},{type:"heading",text:"6. ARM templates"},{type:"p",text:"Now we get into Infrastructure as Code (IaC). Suppose you need to create 5 VMs, 2 storage accounts, 1 virtual network, and 3 subnets. You could click through the portal. That is slow and easy to mess up. Instead, you describe your infrastructure in a template. That is an ARM template: a JSON file that describes the Azure resources you want to deploy."},{type:"diagram",title:"Template in, resources out",ascii:`ARM Template (JSON)
      ↓
"Create a VM"
"Create a Storage Account"
"Create a VNet"
      ↓
Azure Resource Manager
      ↓
Azure resources`},{type:"exam",title:"Why ARM templates show up on the exam",points:["Infrastructure as Code — the setup is written down, not clicked","Repeatable deployments","Consistent environments (dev looks like prod)","Automation and reuse"]},{type:"heading",text:"7. Bicep"},{type:"p",text:"This is probably the easiest one once you know ARM templates. Bicep is a language for deploying Azure resources. It is designed to be easier to write than ARM JSON templates. ARM templates use JSON and can become huge and hard to read. Bicep uses a much cleaner syntax. Under the hood, Bicep still deploys through Azure Resource Manager."},{type:"diagram",title:"Bicep is a friendlier front door to ARM",ascii:`Bicep
  ↓
ARM
  ↓
Azure resources`},{type:"think",text:"Bicep is a simpler, cleaner way to define Azure infrastructure while still using ARM for deployment."},{type:"heading",text:"The relationships that actually matter"},{type:"p",text:"This is the part to lock in for the exam. Management tools talk to ARM. ARM talks to resources. Infrastructure as Code also talks to ARM. Arc is the special case that lets ARM-style management reach outside Azure."},{type:"diagram",title:"How you talk to Azure",ascii:`                    Azure
                      │
              Resource Manager
                    (ARM)
                      │
       ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
       │              │              │
   Azure Portal   Azure CLI     PowerShell
       │              │              │
       └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                      │
                Azure Resources`},{type:"diagram",title:"Infrastructure as Code still uses ARM",ascii:`        Bicep             ARM Template
          │                    │
          └─────────┬──────────┘
                    ↓
             Azure Resource
              Manager (ARM)
                    ↓
             Azure Resources`},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Azure portal",what:"The clickable website for Azure",remember:"GUI in the browser"},{term:"Azure Cloud Shell",what:"Browser-based command-line environment",remember:"Terminal in your browser"},{term:"Azure CLI",what:"Command-line tool",remember:"az commands"},{term:"Azure PowerShell",what:"PowerShell-based management tool",remember:"Az commands like New-Az…"},{term:"Azure Arc",what:"Extends Azure management outside Azure",remember:"Hybrid / multicloud, not a migration tool"},{term:"ARM",what:"Azure Resource Manager",remember:"Azure's management layer"},{term:"ARM templates",what:"JSON Infrastructure as Code",remember:"Define Azure infrastructure in JSON"},{term:"Bicep",what:"Infrastructure-as-Code language",remember:"Cleaner alternative to ARM JSON"}]},{type:"heading",text:"If you see these on the exam, think…"},{type:"steps",items:[{title:"Cloud Shell",text:'"I want a terminal without installing tools."'},{title:"Azure CLI",text:'"I want to type az commands."'},{title:"Azure PowerShell",text:'"I want to use PowerShell commands."'},{title:"Azure Arc",text:'"I want Azure management for resources outside Azure."'},{title:"ARM",text:'"Azure\'s resource management layer."'},{title:"ARM template",text:'"I want to define infrastructure using JSON."'},{title:"Bicep",text:'"I want Infrastructure as Code with a simpler Azure-specific language."'}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Cloud Shell is where you work, CLI and PowerShell are how you give commands, ARM manages the resources, Bicep and ARM templates define what you want deployed, and Azure Arc lets Azure manage resources outside Azure."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"A company has servers in its own datacenter and wants to manage them with Azure Policy and Azure tags, without moving those servers into Azure.",answer:"Azure Arc",why:"Arc extends Azure management to machines that stay outside Azure. It is not a lift-and-shift tool."}],quiz:[{id:"md-1",prompt:"You want to run Azure CLI commands from a browser without installing anything on your laptop. What should you use?",options:["Azure Arc","Azure Cloud Shell","An ARM template","Azure Advisor"],answer:1,explanation:"Cloud Shell is a terminal in the browser. It already has Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell ready to use.",trap:"Arc is for managing resources outside Azure. Advisor gives recommendations. ARM templates describe infrastructure."},{id:"md-2",prompt:"Which tool uses commands that start with az?",options:["Azure PowerShell","Azure CLI","Azure Resource Manager","Azure Monitor"],answer:1,explanation:"Azure CLI commands look like az group create. PowerShell looks like New-AzResourceGroup. ARM is the layer underneath, not a command style."},{id:"md-3",prompt:"What is Azure Resource Manager (ARM)?",options:["A JSON file that describes a virtual machine","A browser-based terminal","The management layer that processes create, update, and delete requests for Azure resources","A tool for moving on-premises servers into Azure"],answer:2,explanation:"ARM is Azure's control plane. Portal, CLI, PowerShell, templates, and Bicep all go through it."},{id:"md-4",prompt:"A team wants repeatable, consistent deployments described as code, but they find ARM JSON templates hard to read. What should they use?",options:["Azure Arc","Azure Cloud Shell","Bicep","Azure Advisor"],answer:2,explanation:"Bicep is a cleaner language that still deploys through ARM. It is the friendlier alternative to ARM JSON templates."},{id:"md-5",prompt:"You need to manage servers that live in another cloud using Azure management tools. Which service is designed for that?",options:["Azure Cloud Shell","Azure Arc","Azure Virtual Desktop","Availability zones"],answer:1,explanation:"Azure Arc extends Azure management to resources outside Azure, including other clouds and on-premises."},{id:"md-6",prompt:"New-AzResourceGroup is an example of which tool?",options:["Azure CLI","Azure PowerShell","Bicep","Azure Resource Manager templates"],answer:1,explanation:"PowerShell uses Verb-Noun cmdlets such as New-AzResourceGroup. CLI would be az group create."},{id:"md-7",prompt:"ARM templates are described as declarative. What does that mean?",options:["You describe the end state you want, and Azure works out how to create it","You write every step in order, like a script","Templates can only be deployed from the portal","Templates delete existing resources first"],answer:0,explanation:"Declarative = say WHAT you want, not HOW to do it. ARM handles ordering and dependencies."},{id:"md-8",prompt:"Which shells can you choose in Azure Cloud Shell?",options:["Bash and PowerShell","Only Command Prompt","Only Bash","Only the Azure portal UI"],answer:0,explanation:"Cloud Shell offers Bash and PowerShell, with Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell preinstalled."},{id:"md-9",prompt:"Which benefit of infrastructure as code is MOST relevant when you must create identical dev, test, and production environments?",options:["Repeatable, consistent deployments from the same template","Free compute","Automatic MFA for all users","Lower egress prices"],answer:0,explanation:"IaC lets you deploy the same template many times with the same result, and keep it in source control."},{id:"md-10",prompt:"Which of these can Azure Arc manage?",options:["Servers, Kubernetes clusters, and data services running outside Azure","Only Azure virtual machines","Only Microsoft 365 mailboxes","Only physical network switches"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure Arc extends Azure management to on-premises and multi-cloud servers, Kubernetes clusters, and data services."}]},{slug:"monitoring",order:16,title:"Monitoring Azure: Advisor, Service Health, and Monitor",emoji:"📡",domain:"governance",minutes:18,summary:"Azure Advisor for recommendations, Azure Service Health for “is my stuff on fire,” and Azure Monitor — Log Analytics, alerts, and Application Insights — for telemetry.",examFocus:"Advisor suggests. Service Health is personalized to your subscriptions (Status is the public page). Monitor collects metrics and logs; Log Analytics queries them; alerts notify; Application Insights watches your app.",youWillLearn:["The five recommendation categories in Azure Advisor","Service Health vs Resource Health vs the public Azure Status page","What Azure Monitor is, including Log Analytics and alerts","When Application Insights is the right name on a question"],officialModule:{...o.monitoring,units:["Describe the purpose of Azure Advisor","Describe Azure Service Health","Describe Azure Monitor"]},blocks:[{type:"lead",emoji:"📡",text:"Azure can recommend better setups, tell you when Microsoft is having a bad day that actually hits you, and collect the numbers and logs your apps produce. Those are three different products. The exam will offer all three as answers to the same story. We will give each one a job description."},{type:"p",text:"A hospital analogy helps. Azure Advisor is the consultant who walks the wards and says “this bed could be cheaper, this door should be locked.” Azure Service Health is the hospital operator telling you which of your rooms are affected by a water outage. Azure Monitor is the banks of screens: heart rate, temperature, alarms, and a specialist who only watches one patient (your application)."},{type:"heading",text:"1. Azure Advisor"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Advisor looks at your resources and produces personalized recommendations. It does not deploy them for you unless you choose to act. For AZ-900, memorize the five categories: Cost, Security, Reliability, Operational excellence, and Performance. (You may also see Sustainability in the product; the exam still hangs on those five.) Advisor is not an outage dashboard and it is not a log query tool."},{type:"list",items:["Cost — unused public IPs, idle VMs, reservations you could buy","Security — follow-up on hardening (Defender for Cloud often feeds this story)","Reliability — make things survive failure (zones, backups, redundancy)","Operational excellence — follow good practices, subscriptions, notifications","Performance — SKUs, caching, throughput that is too small"]},{type:"think",text:"Advisor = “here is how to run Azure better.” If the scenario is an ongoing Microsoft outage, Advisor is the wrong answer."},{type:"heading",text:"2. Azure Service Health"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Service Health is personalized. It knows your subscriptions and tells you about issues that affect you: service problems, planned maintenance, health advisories, and security advisories. You can set up alerts so someone is paged when Microsoft posts an incident on a service you actually use. Resource Health is the sibling view for one resource: “is this particular VM healthy right now, and was it Microsoft's fault?” The public Azure Status page is different — a global billboard for everyone, not tailored to your account."},{type:"compare",items:[{title:"Azure Status",subtitle:"Public billboard",points:["status.azure.com (and similar public views)","Worldwide picture of Azure","Not customized to your subscriptions"]},{title:"Azure Service Health",subtitle:"Your subscriptions",points:["Service issues that hit you","Planned maintenance","Health and security advisories","You can alert on it"]},{title:"Resource Health",subtitle:"This one resource",points:["Is this VM / SQL / app healthy?","Helps tell platform vs your configuration","Zooms in from Service Health"]}]},{type:"trap",text:"Azure Status is not Service Health. Status = everyone. Service Health = you. If the question says “notify us when an Azure outage affects our subscription,” the answer is Service Health (with alerts), not the public Status page and not Advisor."},{type:"heading",text:"3. Azure Monitor"},{type:"p",text:"Azure Monitor is the umbrella for collecting, analyzing, and acting on telemetry. Metrics are numerical measurements over time (CPU %, request count). Logs are records of events. Monitor sits in the middle: data comes in from Azure resources, from guests, from apps; you chart it, query it, alert on it, and optionally send it out."},{type:"diagram",title:"Monitor is the hub",ascii:` Apps, VMs, Azure resources
            │  metrics + logs
            ▼
      Azure Monitor
       /    |     \\
      /     |      \\
 Log        Alerts   Application
 Analytics           Insights
 (query)    (notify) (your app's APM)`},{type:"heading",text:"4. Log Analytics"},{type:"p",text:"Log Analytics is the workspace where logs live and where you query them (the language is Kusto Query Language, or KQL — you do not need to write KQL for AZ-900). Think of it as the searchable archive: “show me failed sign-ins,” “show me disk errors on these VMs.” The workspace is the container. Azure Monitor uses it. Many other services send data into it."},{type:"heading",text:"5. Azure Monitor alerts"},{type:"p",text:"Alerts are the “tap someone on the shoulder” piece. A condition fires — CPU over 90% for 10 minutes, a log query returns failures, a Service Health incident starts — and an action group emails, SMS, or calls a webhook. Alerts are not recommendations (that is Advisor) and they are not the public Status page. They are rules you define, watching your data."},{type:"heading",text:"6. Application Insights"},{type:"p",text:"Application Insights is Azure Monitor's Application Performance Management (APM) feature. You point it at your web app or service and it tracks request rates, response times, failures, dependencies, and live metrics. If the question is about an application — “why is our website slow, where are the exceptions” — Application Insights is the named answer. If the question is about an Azure region outage, it is Service Health. If it is about unused disks, it is Advisor."},{type:"exam",title:"AZ-900 exam points",points:["Advisor: Cost, Security, Reliability, Operational excellence, Performance","Service Health: your issues, maintenance, advisories — not the public Status page","Resource Health: this one resource","Azure Monitor: metrics + logs hub","Log Analytics: query logs in a workspace","Alerts: notify when a condition is true","Application Insights: watch the application's health and performance"]},{type:"cheatsheet",rows:[{term:"Azure Advisor",what:"Personalized recommendations",remember:"Five categories — not an outage feed"},{term:"Azure Status",what:"Public Azure health page",remember:"Everyone, not just you"},{term:"Service Health",what:"Health for your subscriptions",remember:"Issues, maintenance, advisories"},{term:"Resource Health",what:"Health of one resource",remember:"This VM, right now"},{term:"Azure Monitor",what:"Telemetry platform",remember:"Metrics, logs, hub of the rest"},{term:"Log Analytics",what:"Log workspace + queries",remember:"Search the archive"},{term:"Alerts",what:"Rules + action groups",remember:"Notify a person or system"},{term:"Application Insights",what:"APM for your app",remember:"Requests, failures, performance"}]},{type:"steps",items:[{title:"Save money or tighten a VM size",text:"Azure Advisor (Cost / Performance)."},{title:"Email us if Microsoft has an incident on our SQL servers",text:"Azure Service Health alerts."},{title:"Is this one disk or VM unhealthy?",text:"Resource Health."},{title:"Query logs across many resources",text:"Log Analytics (Azure Monitor)."},{title:"Page on-call when CPU is high",text:"Azure Monitor alerts."},{title:"Our web app is slow and throwing exceptions",text:"Application Insights."}]},{type:"onesentence",text:"Advisor recommends how to run Azure better, Service Health tells you when Microsoft's issues (or maintenance) hit your subscriptions — unlike the public Status page — and Azure Monitor is the telemetry hub with Log Analytics to query, alerts to notify, and Application Insights to watch your app."},{type:"scenario",title:"Exam-style scene",setup:"An operations lead wants two things: a weekly list of unused public IP addresses to delete, and a text message if Azure reports an outage that affects this company's subscriptions.",answer:"Azure Advisor for the unused IPs (Cost); Azure Service Health alerts for the outage texts.",why:"Advisor finds waste. Service Health is personalized incident information you can alert on. Application Insights would be for the app's own exceptions. The public Status page would not know which subscriptions are yours."}],quiz:[{id:"mon-1",prompt:"Which of the following is a recommendation category in Azure Advisor?",options:["Conditional Access","Reliability","CanNotDelete","ExpressRoute"],answer:1,explanation:"Advisor's five categories are Cost, Security, Reliability, Operational excellence, and Performance. The others are identity, locks, and networking."},{id:"mon-2",prompt:"You want a personalized view of Azure outages, planned maintenance, and advisories that affect your subscriptions. Which service?",options:["The public Azure Status page only","Azure Service Health","Azure Policy","Microsoft Entra ID"],answer:1,explanation:"Service Health is scoped to your subscriptions. Azure Status is a public, global page and is not personalized.",trap:"Status = everyone. Service Health = you."},{id:"mon-3",prompt:"A single virtual machine looks unhealthy and you want to know if the Azure platform is the cause. Where do you look first?",options:["Azure Pricing Calculator","Resource Health","Microsoft Purview","A ReadOnly lock"],answer:1,explanation:"Resource Health is the per-resource view. Service Health is the broader personalized incident feed. Purview is data governance."},{id:"mon-4",prompt:"Where do you run queries against collected logs in Azure Monitor?",options:["Azure Cloud Shell only","Log Analytics","Azure Arc","The Pricing Calculator"],answer:1,explanation:"Log Analytics workspaces store logs and you query them there (KQL). Cloud Shell is a terminal. Arc extends management outside Azure."},{id:"mon-5",prompt:"Developers want request rates, response times, and exception traces for their web app. Which feature?",options:["Application Insights","Azure Blueprints","Resource locks","Microsoft Entra Domain Services"],answer:0,explanation:"Application Insights is APM inside Azure Monitor. It is the named tool for application performance and failures."},{id:"mon-6",prompt:"You need an email when CPU on a VM stays above 90% for 15 minutes. What do you create?",options:["An Azure Policy initiative","An Azure Monitor alert (with an action group)","A CanNotDelete lock","A B2B guest invitation"],answer:1,explanation:"That is a metric alert in Azure Monitor, delivered through an action group. Policy enforces configuration. Locks prevent delete. Guest invites are identity."},{id:"mon-7",prompt:"Which three experiences make up Azure Service Health?",options:["Azure status, Service Health, and Resource Health","Advisor, Policy, and Locks","Log Analytics, alerts, and Application Insights","Portal, CLI, and PowerShell"],answer:0,explanation:"Azure status is the global public view, Service Health is personalized to your subscriptions and regions, and Resource Health is per resource."},{id:"mon-8",prompt:"Which website shows the health of all Azure services globally, without signing in?",options:["Azure status","Resource Health","Azure Advisor","Azure Monitor alerts"],answer:0,explanation:"The Azure status page is public and global. Service Health and Resource Health are personalized and require signing in."},{id:"mon-9",prompt:"Azure Monitor collects which two fundamental types of data?",options:["Metrics and logs","Invoices and budgets","Users and groups","Tags and locks"],answer:0,explanation:"Metrics are numeric values over time (CPU %); logs are records of events. Azure Monitor collects both from Azure, on-premises, and other clouds."},{id:"mon-10",prompt:"Azure Advisor recommends enabling MFA for admin accounts. Which Advisor category is that?",options:["Security","Cost","Performance","Operational excellence"],answer:0,explanation:"Advisor's five categories are Reliability, Security, Performance, Operational excellence, and Cost. MFA is a Security recommendation."}]}].sort((e,t)=>e.order-t.order);e.s(["getAdjacent",0,function(e){let t=a.findIndex(t=>t.slug===e);return{prev:t>0?a[t-1]:void 0,next:t>=0&&t<a.length-1?a[t+1]:void 0}},"getLesson",0,function(e){return a.find(t=>t.slug===e)},"lessons",0,a,"lessonsForDomain",0,function(e){return a.filter(t=>t.domain===e)}],80873)}]);